00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:32.260
So capital for most of
you who have engaged

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:38.790
with the text quite
extensively already know

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.780
that it is of course
about the working class

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:43.713
and hence it is about labor,

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:49.130
but this claim can appear
a bit controversial

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:51.990
because barring a few passages

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.897
and the very famous
chapter on the working day,

00:06:54.897 --> 00:06:59.640
"Capital" as a text is actually
predominantly concerned

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:04.070
with abstract labor or
the value form of labor.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:06.850
So what do I mean about
the value form of labor?

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:09.750
Meaning that the text talks not so much

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:14.296
about work or labor,
except that famous chapter,

00:07:14.296 --> 00:07:18.244
but about labor power,
the capacity to labor,

00:07:18.244 --> 00:07:20.010
which is what the worker

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:22.450
is forced to sell to the capitalist.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:25.620
And the text also spends
a lot of time discussing

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.421
how that labor power through

00:07:27.421 --> 00:07:30.307
a rather torturous process turns

00:07:30.307 --> 00:07:32.543
into surplus value or profit.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:35.670
But since it is capital

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:39.850
that best teaches us that
the value form violates

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:44.152
and extinguishes all sensuous
properties of human labor,

00:07:44.152 --> 00:07:46.250
Marx' central concern

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:50.280
in Capital appears to be
tracing the procedures

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.740
of that extinguishment rather

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.990
than establishing the subjective

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:57.573
conscious aspects of human labor.

00:07:58.736 --> 00:08:01.400
This particular reading of "Capital"

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:04.990
as a text organized
solely around the formed

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:09.590
determination of wage
labor has actually led

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:13.900
some Marxists to make a
further analytical leap

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.730
about the indifference if you like

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:19.193
of capitalism as a mode of production.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.940
So for instance, Ellen Meiksins Wood,

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:26.350
a prominent advocate of
this view has argued,

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.870
Audrey, could we have
the first slide please?

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:29.703
Thank you.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:32.632
So it's the next slide.

00:08:32.632 --> 00:08:34.240
Thank you.

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:37.160
So Ellen Wood has argued that sexual

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:40.150
and racial equality are not

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:44.050
in principle incompatible with capitalism.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:47.650
The disappearance of class
inequality on the other hand

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:51.020
is by definition
incompatible with capitalism.

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:52.985
So in other words

00:08:52.985 --> 00:08:57.985
we can have a capitalism where there is

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:02.430
no sexual or racial inequality inequality

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.740
but we can't have a capitalism

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:07.833
where there's class equality.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:10.540
In this reading of Marx,

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:13.630
capitalism as a system is reconstructed

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:17.600
as the first and only exploitive system

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:19.720
that does not require the production

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:24.720
and sustenance of extra
economic inequalities.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.310
The existence of such inequalities is seen

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:30.030
as contingent rather than exigent

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:33.660
to the system whose reproduction
can be assured simply

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:36.140
through the wage form or
through the extraction

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:39.663
of surplus value between
economically unequal

00:09:39.663 --> 00:09:42.653
but juridically equal subjects.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:46.140
Social Reproduction Theory,

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:49.576
a conceptual operator's
primarily developed

00:09:49.576 --> 00:09:52.820
from "Capital" by Marxist feminists.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.960
I will argue today offers a reading

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.340
of capitalism and of "Capital Volume I".

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.230
One that is actually more consistent

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:04.780
with Marx's own method
than this hypothesis

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:07.290
of capitalist indifference.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:08.463
Next slide please.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:11.720
So I'd make this argument

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.740
in three separate, but related ways.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:19.640
First at a general level, by
looking at the status of labor

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:23.840
in "Capital" a second, through
a more specific argument

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:27.010
about capitalism, systemic unity,

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:31.100
and this sort of hearkens
back to the quote

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:36.100
that Paul used from my essay earlier.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:41.380
And finally through an empirical argument

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:43.610
about how it is exactly that labor

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:47.100
power is differentiated
dividing the working class

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:48.463
into distinct sectors.

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:53.350
So let me preface my argument
with some very brief remarks

00:10:53.350 --> 00:10:56.450
about Social Reproduction Theory, or SRT,

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:58.593
and its theoretical contours.

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:03.650
Fundamentally SRT theorizes labor power,

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.680
and the practices that
enable its reproduction

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.500
within capitalist social relations,

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:12.590
situated within and following

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:16.790
from the conceptual
architecture of "Capital",

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:20.773
SRT proposes that, next slide please.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:25.330
One, the labor expended

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:28.140
in the production of
commodities that is at the point

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:31.500
of production and the labor
expended for the production

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:35.342
of people or workers who produce
such commodities are part

00:11:35.342 --> 00:11:38.188
of the same capitalist totality

00:11:38.188 --> 00:11:42.430
and together constitutive of value.

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:44.520
And the second proposal is

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:47.172
that the working class
family is the primary

00:11:47.172 --> 00:11:49.890
but not the only site

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:52.890
for the reproduction of labor
power or the reproduction

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:54.493
of the working class as a whole.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:56.703
Further, SRT identifies

00:11:56.703 --> 00:12:00.913
capital's contradictory relationship

00:12:00.913 --> 00:12:05.420
to labor power as a key
feature of the system.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:07.270
How is it contradictory?

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:08.970
Because on the one hand,

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:13.230
capital needs a steady predictable circuit

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:15.790
for the reproduction of labor
power, since it is the source

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:20.470
of capitalist profit and
hence its own reproduction.

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:22.418
So it has to have reliable means

00:12:22.418 --> 00:12:26.160
that labor power is reproduced constantly,

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.210
but on the other hand,

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:30.630
that labor power is actually reproduced

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:34.585
by human beings and hence
how it is reproduced

00:12:34.585 --> 00:12:39.585
to what extent labor powers
capacities are developed

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:42.310
or the human capacities are developed

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:44.740
may not always align with capital's needs

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:50.810
thus all sites and processes
for the reproduction

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:55.057
of labor power are ultimately arenas

00:12:55.057 --> 00:12:57.590
of contestation between labor and capital.

00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:02.310
The former constantly
tending towards greater share

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:07.310
of civilization to develop
and further human capacities.

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.800
So that's labor's job,

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:12.480
while the latter, i.e., capital,

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:15.250
straining to reduce such a share

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:19.370
and mold labor power, and
such capacities to create

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:24.370
and maintain the productive
worker that capital needs.

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:29.300
So let's look at some examples, right?

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:32.420
So think about what kind of

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:38.120
a worker capitalism needs
and how do we attain it.

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:42.054
So in that sense you have

00:13:42.054 --> 00:13:46.680
great to discipline at schools to produce

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:51.680
the perfect subject for
work later on in life.

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:57.590
You have the heteronormative family

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:00.500
as the ideal that capital
is most comfortable

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:03.510
with in order to
reproduce the labor power.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:05.750
So you have all these institutions

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:10.210
but then these institutions
do not always work

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:12.770
as reliably as possible because look,

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:15.850
we're at Yale and we're
teaching "Capital Volume I",

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:19.950
i.e., we are encouraging
you to actually think

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:24.290
about the system rather
than comply with the system.

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:28.640
So there's always this sort
of contradictory relationship

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:32.880
between the processes of the
production of commodities

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.563
and the processes of the
production of labor power.

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:38.323
So slide five, please.

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:44.457
And we can see that this
contestation happens.

00:14:44.457 --> 00:14:45.400
I'm not sure

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:48.511
if you can see both of
the photographs very well,

00:14:48.511 --> 00:14:51.484
that this contestation then can happen

00:14:51.484 --> 00:14:55.370
both at the point of
production and outside of it.

00:14:55.370 --> 00:15:00.370
The first photo is of
homeowners in Chicago,

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:04.700
actually protesting redlining.

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:06.949
And the second is a point of production

00:15:06.949 --> 00:15:11.949
obviously a workers strike at Woolworth's.

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:15.813
And we'll go talk about this more later.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:21.780
So SRT then is at its core concerned

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:26.870
with the gendered racialized
reproduction of labor power,

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:31.920
and "Capital" as text is
fundamentally concerned

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:35.370
with how surplus value is produced

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:39.303
and with what consequences,
then how are the two related?

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:43.970
So positions such as Ellen Woods above

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:45.020
that we talked about,

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:48.070
try to demonstrate that
capitalist social relations

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:51.410
as they unfold historically ought

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:53.730
to be theoretically separated

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:58.730
out from the abstract or logical
architecture of capitalism.

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:02.092
So in this view, you can
have a historic capitalism

00:16:02.092 --> 00:16:06.608
which is how it really
happens, but "Capital Volume I"

00:16:06.608 --> 00:16:10.400
it can be argued is
actually the abstraction

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:13.823
and it's not really about
history or anything.

00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:19.840
So this particular derivation of capital

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:22.750
and approach to capitalism are in my view,

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:27.060
actually at odds with
both Marx's methodology

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:28.653
and the project of Capital.

00:16:30.337 --> 00:16:31.330
"Capital Volume I",

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:34.310
is individually about
the value form of labor.

00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:36.210
And we can certainly find passages

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:38.520
in Marx, which might support the argument

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:42.310
that economic categories ought
to be considered distinctly

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:45.050
from their historic unfolding.

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:49.520
However we cannot lose
sight of the fact that

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:52.660
what distinguishes Marxist method from

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:55.940
non-Marxist ones, say Ricardo's concept

00:16:55.940 --> 00:17:00.453
of fixed essences is that
Marx rejects a separation

00:17:00.453 --> 00:17:04.140
between reality and its apprehension

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:07.851
in thought instead his
methodology establishes

00:17:07.851 --> 00:17:12.020
a dialectical unity between them operating

00:17:12.020 --> 00:17:15.833
simultaneously at both
logical and historical levels.

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:21.890
To be clear, Marx is not
proposing a simple unity

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:25.580
between theory and reality
in any naturalistic way

00:17:25.580 --> 00:17:28.050
but of how theoretical categories

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:32.370
actually derive from
historic developments.

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:33.860
This does not mean that such

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:38.100
categories are reflective
of immediate appearances.

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:41.490
Indeed, as we know, from
our reading of Marx,

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:44.070
they may be they're mirror opposites

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:46.540
rather his method discloses

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:50.040
a constant upward spiral movement

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:54.320
between theory and reality
or appearance and essence

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:56.450
however you want to think about it,

00:17:56.450 --> 00:17:58.020
becoming more complex

00:17:58.020 --> 00:18:00.843
at each successive level of determination.

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:06.470
So this relationship
between "Capital Volume I"

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:11.430
one as a theoretical text and
history or reality existing

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:15.130
outside of the text is often
classed as a relationship

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:19.940
between abstract categories
and concrete histories, right?

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:21.520
So far we've argued

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:24.550
that there is a relationship
between the abstract categories

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:28.346
and concrete history, but
actually Marx goes much further

00:18:28.346 --> 00:18:31.650
than this simple relationality.

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:34.970
So what is an abstract category?

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:37.100
Let's try to unpack that a bit,

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:39.870
an abstract category commonly understood

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:41.650
is a static one, right?

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:42.930
One that doesn't move,

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:46.100
doesn't change with
historical change and so on.

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:50.233
An unmoving part separated
from a dynamic whole

00:18:50.233 --> 00:18:53.730
which can reveal to the
analyst key features

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:58.169
of that whole because that
whole is impossible to study

00:18:58.169 --> 00:19:02.920
in its totality or, or
complete complexity.

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:05.760
Marx himself uses several
such abstractions,

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:10.528
money, commodity being two
of the most common ones

00:19:10.528 --> 00:19:14.470
to understand history and
the nature of capitalism,

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:18.430
but unlike bourgeois
approaches to abstraction,

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:23.430
Marx's methodology
refuses all static forms.

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:28.450
Hence one of the key
theorists, according to me,

00:19:28.450 --> 00:19:31.762
of Marx's method

00:19:31.762 --> 00:19:36.762
which is Bertell Ollman,
Bertell Ollman has argued

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:41.250
that Marx sets out to
abstract things in his words,

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:46.250
as they really are and
happen making how they happen

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:48.770
part of what they are,

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:52.180
hence capital or labor or money

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:55.856
is not only how capital
appears and functions,

00:19:55.856 --> 00:19:58.570
but also how it develops

00:19:58.570 --> 00:20:02.136
or rather how it develops its real history

00:20:02.136 --> 00:20:05.450
is also part of what it is.

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:10.450
So just to sort of do an aside here,

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:15.670
if you remember in the German ideology,

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:18.480
he and Engels mocked Bruno Bauer

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:22.050
for attempting to prove an
antithetical relationship

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:24.791
between nature and history as if,

00:20:24.791 --> 00:20:26.897
and Marx says and I quote,

00:20:26.897 --> 00:20:29.250
"These were two separate things."

00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:30.494
Unquote.

00:20:30.494 --> 00:20:33.800
In contrast, a Marxist abstraction.

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:36.544
So this sort of category that you abstract

00:20:36.544 --> 00:20:39.320
from the whole to understand the whole,

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:42.766
a Marxist abstraction encloses within it,

00:20:42.766 --> 00:20:46.100
both a history of its becoming

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:49.580
while disclosing its possible future.

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:53.715
For instance, within the
abstract category capital

00:20:53.715 --> 00:20:57.060
we also find it's past,

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:00.573
which is the so-called
primitive accumulation as well

00:21:00.573 --> 00:21:05.076
as forecast of its
future constant expansion

00:21:05.076 --> 00:21:07.190
through surplus value extraction,

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:10.930
the accumulation drive, creating
the world market and so on.

00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:15.400
So the past as well as
the future is enclosed

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:19.530
in the abstract category of capital.

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:21.840
So while we might find these categories

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:25.410
in whole or in part in
other epochs of history,

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:27.330
i.e., in other modes of production

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:31.920
like we will find money
or labor and so on.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:35.180
They either remain
unrealized in their function

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:36.940
or play entirely different roles

00:21:36.940 --> 00:21:39.440
from their current incarnation.

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:43.990
When they combine in the
capitalist mode of production,

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:48.809
they do so as being simultaneously
saturated with their past

00:21:48.809 --> 00:21:53.520
while establishing new
relationships within themselves.

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:57.130
So the history of pre-capitalist practices

00:21:57.130 --> 00:22:00.360
thus cannot be separated or severed

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:03.913
from the explanatory
categories of capitalism.

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:09.970
So this is a digression
in how the methodology

00:22:10.424 --> 00:22:15.424
that I think is useful in going
back to reading "Capital".

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:19.242
And once we have this methodology in place

00:22:19.242 --> 00:22:22.100
let us now revisit Ellen Wood's

00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:25.470
claim that the extraction of surplus

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:27.380
value is the primary concern for

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:31.830
capital and hence how
that process happens,

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:35.377
i.e., whether that process happens

00:22:35.377 --> 00:22:39.260
to racialized gendered or ableist means

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:44.150
is actually incidental to
capital's central pursuit, right?

00:22:44.150 --> 00:22:46.540
So remember we are now talking.

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:48.811
So if we use that idea

00:22:48.811 --> 00:22:53.606
then Marxist methodology doesn't

00:22:53.606 --> 00:22:55.750
really apply because as we tried

00:22:55.750 --> 00:22:58.060
to establish that the abstract in

00:22:58.060 --> 00:23:01.120
Marx actually discloses the history

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:04.670
of the category itself and
discloses its unfolding.

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:09.490
So as we've already then established

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:11.210
that even abstract categories

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:13.970
in Marx carry historical lineages

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:16.020
and future illusions,

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:19.320
in a sense, this should
be enough to reject

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:21.830
Woods' claim of capitalist indifference

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:24.960
but there is actually a case to be

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:29.580
made for indifference as
regards to Capitalist forms

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:34.580
not however, in the way that
Ellen Wood conceives of it.

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:37.670
So let us take the abstract category labor

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:41.163
as it appears in Marx,
it is just banally true

00:23:41.163 --> 00:23:43.900
that human beings have always labored

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:47.980
in a diversity of historical
epochs and social formations,

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:51.470
but labor as a simple
category that Marx uses

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:53.880
to build the theoretical infrastructure

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:55.293
of "Capital Volume I",

00:23:56.820 --> 00:24:01.630
when economically conceived
in this simplicity says Marx

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:04.203
it becomes, and I quote,

00:24:05.737 --> 00:24:08.550
"A modern category as the relations

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:11.240
which create this simple abstraction."

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:14.120
So capital, so under capitalism

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:19.120
labor as such, not any
specific kind of labor,

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:23.898
not the sort of act of building a table

00:24:23.898 --> 00:24:28.700
or teaching or cooking,
not any specific kind

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:32.830
of labor, but labor as
such becomes the basis

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:34.730
for the creation of wealth.

00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:37.140
To conceive of labor in this,

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:41.180
its most simple form was
an analytical leap taken

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:43.070
by classical political economy

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:47.210
which actually Marx is
very much in support of

00:24:47.210 --> 00:24:52.210
and absolutely gives props to Adam Smith.

00:24:53.790 --> 00:24:57.780
He's wrote, "It was a
tremendous advance on the part

00:24:57.780 --> 00:25:02.780
of Adam Smith to throw aside
every limiting specification

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:05.580
of wealth creating activity,

00:25:05.580 --> 00:25:07.850
not only manufacturing or commercial

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:11.100
or agricultural labor but one as well

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:13.770
as the others labor in general.

00:25:13.770 --> 00:25:18.770
So Smith first came upon this idea

00:25:18.980 --> 00:25:22.253
and Marx follows very much from it.

00:25:23.210 --> 00:25:26.600
But how did classical
political economy arrive

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:28.833
at this generalized form of labor?

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:33.296
Not simply because laboring
was an historical category,

00:25:33.296 --> 00:25:35.400
common to all epochs,

00:25:35.400 --> 00:25:38.570
but because labor under
capitalism had become

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:41.490
for the first time labor in general.

00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:46.490
So the theoretical conception
of this general or simple form

00:25:46.690 --> 00:25:51.690
of labor was only possible
in the historical actuality

00:25:52.990 --> 00:25:56.747
a very developed totality
of real kinds of labor

00:25:56.747 --> 00:26:00.540
of which no single one was
any longer predominant."

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.053
Unquote, as Marx put it.

00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:06.220
So abstraction in this case,

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:09.340
the abstract category of labor arose

00:26:09.340 --> 00:26:13.543
from the very many
determinations of the concrete,

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:18.420
while the concrete could only be conceived

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:22.490
of as such through the simple abstraction.

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:25.900
So it is only when the system itself,

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:28.580
the historical system
itself was indifferent

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:31.030
to what kind of labor remember

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:34.570
the first like five chapters
of "Capital Volume I", right?

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:36.375
So all of you've gone through it.

00:26:36.375 --> 00:26:41.375
So the most general abstractions
Marx reminds us arise only

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:48.320
in the midst of the richest
possible concrete development.

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:51.890
And I quote Marx, where one thing appears

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:55.650
as common to many to all then it ceases

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:59.810
to be thinkable in a
particular form alone, right?

00:26:59.810 --> 00:27:02.750
So it was that historical development

00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:06.060
and the concrete unfolding of it that

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:09.680
allowed classical political
economists to have

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:14.200
to make that analytical
leap towards abstraction.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:15.310
So you can see here

00:27:15.310 --> 00:27:18.240
I'm sort of building this
argument that the concrete

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:21.060
and the abstract actually
have a relationship

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:23.030
in Marx in the methodology.

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:25.690
So now you can see where
I will go with this

00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:30.581
with whether the creation of
surplus value is indifferent

00:27:30.581 --> 00:27:33.350
to how surplus value is produced, right?

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:35.360
So that's the argument we are leading

00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:37.770
up to with all this discussion

00:27:37.770 --> 00:27:40.110
on the abstract and the concrete.

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:44.680
It is because indifference
in real sense is expressed

00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:47.305
in the historical form
that it can be grasped

00:27:47.305 --> 00:27:50.700
as such in the abstract theoretical form.

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:54.140
It is because individuals
in capitalist society

00:27:54.140 --> 00:27:56.987
can fluidly move from one
form of labor to another

00:27:56.987 --> 00:28:01.850
that both the category labor and labor

00:28:01.850 --> 00:28:05.997
in reality has become, and I quote Marx,

00:28:05.997 --> 00:28:07.790
"The means of creating wealth

00:28:07.790 --> 00:28:11.010
in general and has ceased
to be organically linked

00:28:11.878 --> 00:28:15.270
to particular individuals
in any specific form.

00:28:15.270 --> 00:28:20.140
Hence, far from there being
an abstract capitalism,

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:21.979
indifferent to historical forms

00:28:21.979 --> 00:28:25.240
and historical capitalism marked

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:27.550
by concrete social relations,

00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:31.140
Marx's methodology urges critical thought

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:34.590
to unite theory and reality

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:38.260
in actually transformatively
radical ways, right?

00:28:38.260 --> 00:28:41.310
So we don't need to have this sort

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:44.680
of historical capitalism
versus abstract capitalism.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:46.590
We should actually think

00:28:46.590 --> 00:28:49.780
about Marxist methodology
challenges us to think

00:28:49.780 --> 00:28:54.100
about rejecting this sort
of a division in thinking,

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:56.660
but think about a dialectical unity

00:28:56.660 --> 00:28:59.683
within the categories themselves.

00:29:01.010 --> 00:29:05.637
So accordingly, Marx posits abstract labor

00:29:05.637 --> 00:29:08.220
in "Capital Volume I".

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:10.570
And you are all very aware of this,

00:29:10.570 --> 00:29:14.280
very carefully and he posits it

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:19.250
as one aspect of his
labor theory of value,

00:29:19.250 --> 00:29:21.860
subjective forms are concrete labor,

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:25.020
the analytical twin of abstract labor,

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:27.120
and social labor through which

00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:31.050
the value form is organized
are all understood

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:36.050
as different aspects of labor
operating as a unity, right?

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:40.700
So most importantly, "Capital Volume I" is

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:45.700
about why and how abstract labor dominates

00:29:47.143 --> 00:29:52.143
over the other aspects and
submits them to the law of value.

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:54.660
So that's really, but it doesn't mean

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:56.950
that there is an abstract
labor that we study

00:29:56.950 --> 00:29:59.640
about and then there's concrete labor.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:01.898
It actually shows you why abstract

00:30:01.898 --> 00:30:05.303
labor dominates over concrete labor.

00:30:06.410 --> 00:30:08.780
If the other aspects

00:30:08.780 --> 00:30:11.930
of labor like concrete
labor and social labor,

00:30:11.930 --> 00:30:16.160
appear as muted shadows of abstract labor,

00:30:16.160 --> 00:30:18.790
then it is not because Marx is reporting

00:30:18.790 --> 00:30:21.230
on reality in any simple way.

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:22.390
These other categories

00:30:22.390 --> 00:30:25.010
in capital have a much
more significant role

00:30:25.010 --> 00:30:28.143
to play in the totality
of Marx's argument.

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:31.100
Concrete labor, or social labor.

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:33.690
Actually these theoretical

00:30:33.690 --> 00:30:35.810
categories actually signal

00:30:35.810 --> 00:30:40.699
the limits to capitalism's
ability to reduce workers

00:30:40.699 --> 00:30:45.283
to simply being potential
sources of surplus value.

00:30:47.082 --> 00:30:48.810
Okay, so we should think
about these categories

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:53.265
not simply as explaining
how surplus value is created

00:30:53.265 --> 00:30:56.347
but also these categories
signal the limits

00:30:56.347 --> 00:30:59.773
to what capital can extract from us.

00:31:00.730 --> 00:31:02.900
So let us look more closely then

00:31:02.900 --> 00:31:04.700
at the relationship between abstract

00:31:04.700 --> 00:31:08.283
and concrete labor and their
respective roles in capitalism.

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:11.310
A system organized on the basis

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:14.180
of abstract labor as we argued before,

00:31:14.180 --> 00:31:16.530
can only appear when different forms

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:18.144
of concrete labor performed

00:31:18.144 --> 00:31:21.420
by historically situated people

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:23.280
are forced into a relationship

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:25.690
of equivalence via the market,

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:27.860
production for which takes place solely

00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:30.393
for profit rather than for human need.

00:31:31.290 --> 00:31:34.360
So the system reproduces
itself through the production

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:38.218
of commodities, a process
involving abstract labor

00:31:38.218 --> 00:31:40.482
or labor in its value form

00:31:40.482 --> 00:31:44.220
but constantly rubbing
against this process

00:31:44.220 --> 00:31:48.185
while nested within it, human
beings reproduce their lives

00:31:48.185 --> 00:31:52.835
through concrete,
historically laden actions,

00:31:52.835 --> 00:31:55.991
reproduction of life-making practices,

00:31:55.991 --> 00:31:58.810
and the reproduction of value

00:31:58.810 --> 00:32:02.520
must be simultaneous and continuous.

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:06.010
Which is the reality of capitalism,

00:32:06.010 --> 00:32:09.635
but also a sense of anxiety for capital.

00:32:09.635 --> 00:32:12.400
Because it does not have the kind

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:14.700
of control it has at the point

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:17.620
of production over the
production of commodities.

00:32:17.620 --> 00:32:20.653
It doesn't have the same
control over the production

00:32:20.653 --> 00:32:25.080
of life-making or a reproduction
of life-making if you like,

00:32:25.080 --> 00:32:27.400
because it doesn't happen
at the point of production.

00:32:27.400 --> 00:32:28.853
So slide six, please.

00:32:31.090 --> 00:32:32.930
Two preconditions attend

00:32:32.930 --> 00:32:35.880
to such a system that is capitalism.

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:40.040
One, that people are forcibly torn

00:32:40.040 --> 00:32:42.183
from the means of
production, such that access

00:32:42.183 --> 00:32:46.550
to their subsistence or
life-making is only available

00:32:46.550 --> 00:32:48.720
through the mechanism of the market.

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:51.040
And second, if the system is able

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:53.248
to ensure its continued reproduction

00:32:53.248 --> 00:32:57.260
by reproducing the social
relations that scaffold

00:32:57.260 --> 00:33:00.290
the market and the
extraction of surplus value.

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:02.170
In other words, capitalist social

00:33:02.170 --> 00:33:04.207
relations must continually reproduce

00:33:04.207 --> 00:33:07.420
the worker's dependence on capital.

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:09.470
So both things as you can see here

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:13.867
in this wonderful feminist
graphic from the 1970s,

00:33:13.867 --> 00:33:17.270
both things are happening
at the same time,

00:33:17.270 --> 00:33:20.130
the woman is split down in the middle,

00:33:20.130 --> 00:33:25.130
to my left she is performing
labor in the domestic sphere.

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:35.150
Many of you listening in
can identify with that,

00:33:35.150 --> 00:33:40.150
especially during pandemic
times of your children at home

00:33:40.660 --> 00:33:45.660
and pulling at your
skirt, and on my right.

00:33:46.160 --> 00:33:50.320
She is also working in the
workplace and you can see

00:33:50.320 --> 00:33:53.340
I always find this very interesting

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:55.600
because there is a there is a clock

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:58.153
that marks time in the workplace.

00:33:59.038 --> 00:34:03.197
So it's a wonderful image
that shows both sides

00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:09.083
of capital, how capital
reproduces itself as a system.

00:34:10.210 --> 00:34:15.160
So the dominance of abstract
over concrete labor of value,

00:34:15.160 --> 00:34:19.110
overused value create the conditions for,

00:34:19.110 --> 00:34:21.170
and necessitate the production

00:34:21.170 --> 00:34:25.190
of a worker compliant
to capital's every whim.

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:29.085
Okay, because if there
isn't a compliant worker

00:34:29.085 --> 00:34:31.010
then there would be no system.

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:33.880
So that's capital's need, right?

00:34:33.880 --> 00:34:37.410
But if this were the whole
story of "Capital Volume I",

00:34:37.410 --> 00:34:40.520
then celebration of its 150th birthday

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:42.033
would be held at Wall Street.

00:34:43.240 --> 00:34:48.240
But how, if at all is then
resistance part of this story.

00:34:49.240 --> 00:34:51.900
If Marxist theory requires all parts

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:54.109
of a social totality to be connected

00:34:54.109 --> 00:34:55.938
through internal relations

00:34:55.938 --> 00:35:00.349
how can resistance to capital
be shown to be imminent

00:35:00.349 --> 00:35:02.800
to the reproduction of capital, right?

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:05.130
Because resistance can't
be from the outside.

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:07.020
It can't be in external relations.

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:10.550
That's going against Marxist methodology.

00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:14.280
And this is where to go back to our point,

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:16.350
Social Reproduction Theory

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:18.930
deploying capital's methodology,

00:35:18.930 --> 00:35:21.640
but building upon some of its silences,

00:35:21.640 --> 00:35:25.390
effects a category
transformation of the concept

00:35:25.390 --> 00:35:28.970
of labor power as the analytical causeway,

00:35:28.970 --> 00:35:33.060
the analytical bridge between
capitalist reproduction

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:35.653
and anticapitalist resistance.

00:35:37.070 --> 00:35:42.070
So even the most creative
reading of the concept

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:46.300
of labor power and capital shows,

00:35:46.300 --> 00:35:49.980
and this is the feminist big
fight with "Capital Volume I"

00:35:49.980 --> 00:35:53.886
one that the text
presupposes the existence

00:35:53.886 --> 00:35:56.620
of what is being reproduced.

00:35:56.620 --> 00:36:00.770
So labor power is just
there in "Capital Volume I".

00:36:00.770 --> 00:36:04.180
Theoretically, it's a
category that's just there.

00:36:04.180 --> 00:36:06.380
Discussion of labor power begins not

00:36:06.380 --> 00:36:10.360
from where or how it has
been societally produced

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:13.540
but the ways in which
in its commodity form

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:15.933
it sustains the production
of surplus value.

00:36:16.960 --> 00:36:20.190
Differently put,
conceptualization of labor power

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:22.860
in the text is limited to the form

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:26.330
in which it is useful to capital.

00:36:26.330 --> 00:36:30.800
SRT advocates this capital-oriented
view of labor power

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:34.151
and performs in theory,
a reverse movement away

00:36:34.151 --> 00:36:36.830
from the site of commodity production

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:40.113
to the site of the
reproduction of labor power.

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:43.763
So labor power, for those of you,

00:36:44.603 --> 00:36:45.583
who've read the text,

00:36:46.727 --> 00:36:49.623
I'm talking about the
students in the seminar,

00:36:50.670 --> 00:36:53.250
you know that it is nominated variously

00:36:53.250 --> 00:36:58.250
in capital as unique,
special, peculiar commodity

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:02.690
because it is presented
as the only commodity

00:37:02.690 --> 00:37:04.580
and in Marx's famous words,

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:08.490
whose use value possesses
the peculiar property

00:37:08.490 --> 00:37:10.440
of being a source of value.

00:37:10.440 --> 00:37:15.196
And I'm so excited to see how Paul Reitter

00:37:15.196 --> 00:37:18.450
and Paul North will
translate that passage.

00:37:18.450 --> 00:37:20.823
I'm really excited to learn from that.

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:25.320
But SRT reveals that
there is another aspect

00:37:25.320 --> 00:37:28.130
to labor powers peculiarity.

00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:30.540
It is a commodity undoubtedly

00:37:30.540 --> 00:37:35.280
but it is a commodity that is
not produced capitalistically,

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:38.080
that is within the direct ambit

00:37:38.080 --> 00:37:41.560
of the capital wage
labor relations, right?

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:43.493
So it's produced outside of it.

00:37:46.460 --> 00:37:50.710
The feminist who pioneered
some of this thinking,

00:37:50.710 --> 00:37:54.020
Lisa Vogel, has shown that it is produced

00:37:54.020 --> 00:37:57.800
and reproduced what Lisa Vogel calls

00:37:57.800 --> 00:38:01.877
a kin-based site, which is
the working class family.

00:38:01.877 --> 00:38:05.360
So this insight is crucial

00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:08.630
and it is crucial in
three important respects.

00:38:08.630 --> 00:38:13.410
First, because it expresses
the dialectical unity

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:15.750
of what we had argued previously

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:19.380
between the historical and
logical form of capital, right?

00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:24.006
Labor power, SRT shows, can
only become available to capital

00:38:24.006 --> 00:38:29.006
through distinct, but reliable
sets of gendered, racialized,

00:38:29.549 --> 00:38:32.382
social relations that create their

00:38:32.382 --> 00:38:35.600
own institutional forms of sustenance,

00:38:35.600 --> 00:38:39.513
such as the monogamous
heteronormative family forum.

00:38:40.360 --> 00:38:43.250
Older historical forms maybe recast,

00:38:43.250 --> 00:38:46.020
new forums may be created and mobilized,

00:38:46.020 --> 00:38:48.890
but it is only through
concrete, social relations

00:38:48.890 --> 00:38:51.400
and centers human labor,

00:38:51.400 --> 00:38:55.289
that labor power is produced
and made available to capital.

00:38:55.289 --> 00:38:58.800
Second, being attentive to labor power,

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:01.670
not as it appears before capital,

00:39:01.670 --> 00:39:06.670
but as it is produced and
reproduced SRT introduces,

00:39:07.130 --> 00:39:08.890
or rather restores,

00:39:08.890 --> 00:39:13.480
to capitalists totality a
sphere of social relations

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:16.800
where life-making activities proliferate.

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:20.110
While capital's goal is
to increase surplus value

00:39:20.110 --> 00:39:21.900
in the sphere of production.

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:25.620
The worker's goal is to
enhance her quality of life,

00:39:25.620 --> 00:39:28.990
for qualitative enrichment
of her labor power

00:39:28.990 --> 00:39:33.210
and herself would through the
satisfaction of her needs.

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:37.080
SRT reminds us of the power of these life

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:41.980
making activities by workers
for they seek to impose breaks

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:46.150
on the process of accumulation
as capital is forced to pay

00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:49.580
for them through wages
and social benefits.

00:39:49.580 --> 00:39:54.580
So let's go back and look
at this picture, right?

00:39:54.740 --> 00:39:59.740
So look at what is to my
left side of the graphic,

00:40:01.610 --> 00:40:06.350
which is one way that this woman worker

00:40:09.470 --> 00:40:12.210
could enhance the quality of the left side

00:40:12.210 --> 00:40:17.210
of her work sphere is by
having public childcare.

00:40:19.494 --> 00:40:24.494
The crying child on the
floor would actually be

00:40:25.170 --> 00:40:30.170
at a public well-funded
government childcare institution.

00:40:30.820 --> 00:40:33.680
And you can see that she's ironing

00:40:33.680 --> 00:40:36.700
so we could have public
laundries and so on.

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:40.170
And so if on the right
side of the graphic,

00:40:40.170 --> 00:40:43.320
she went on strike to demand that

00:40:43.320 --> 00:40:45.780
then that is actually again,

00:40:45.780 --> 00:40:48.780
putting a break on the
accumulation process

00:40:48.780 --> 00:40:51.410
for her to enhance the
quality of her life.

00:40:51.410 --> 00:40:55.090
So you see there is a relationship
between the production

00:40:55.090 --> 00:40:59.343
of commodity and the
reproduction of labor power.

00:41:00.340 --> 00:41:02.040
So finally, there is a clear

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:04.290
strategic component to SRT.

00:41:04.290 --> 00:41:07.090
Life-making social practices
by workers are not simply

00:41:07.090 --> 00:41:12.090
conjurers of activities to satisfy needs.

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:15.010
They have the potential to carry

00:41:15.010 --> 00:41:17.410
an anticapitalist charge,

00:41:17.410 --> 00:41:19.070
struggles to enhance

00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:23.510
or expand such practices will
always reveal the balance

00:41:23.510 --> 00:41:27.150
of class forces, that I want
better public health care.

00:41:27.150 --> 00:41:30.130
In fact, I want my healthcare to be free.

00:41:30.130 --> 00:41:32.570
I want education to be free.

00:41:32.570 --> 00:41:35.330
This is not to claim that
any, or all struggles

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:37.023
by workers to improve their living

00:41:37.023 --> 00:41:40.833
conditions are all
anti-systemic struggles,

00:41:42.310 --> 00:41:44.690
but it is to argue

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:51.380
that they carry that the potential

00:41:51.380 --> 00:41:56.380
for that sort of anticapitalist charge.

00:41:59.870 --> 00:42:03.670
The struggles that are
about enhancing the sphere

00:42:03.670 --> 00:42:07.390
of life-making for workers can
thus reveal often implicitly

00:42:07.390 --> 00:42:10.570
but sometimes explicitly the upper limits

00:42:10.570 --> 00:42:13.520
to what the working class
can claim in its goal

00:42:13.520 --> 00:42:16.280
for self enhancement and liberation,

00:42:16.280 --> 00:42:19.147
thus they carry the potential
of teaching the worker

00:42:19.147 --> 00:42:21.760
and struggle the most important

00:42:21.760 --> 00:42:24.630
political lesson about capitalism,

00:42:24.630 --> 00:42:25.970
that while the wage

00:42:25.970 --> 00:42:30.504
for life-making activities
can never be free of the drive

00:42:30.504 --> 00:42:34.159
for accumulation and thus
real emancipation life

00:42:34.159 --> 00:42:36.395
and labor is only possible

00:42:36.395 --> 00:42:39.560
with the abolition of
the wage form, right?

00:42:39.560 --> 00:42:42.303
So we can demand for instance,

00:42:42.303 --> 00:42:45.670
that we have free abortion on demand.

00:42:45.670 --> 00:42:50.053
We have free medical
care or free education.

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:53.983
And in certain contexts
capital will even grant that

00:42:53.983 --> 00:42:58.983
but immediately try to claw
it back when it can, right?

00:42:59.050 --> 00:43:04.050
So again, this sort of
struggle to constantly win back

00:43:05.170 --> 00:43:08.110
from capital, our life-making activities

00:43:08.110 --> 00:43:11.720
or our potential for expanded
life-making activities

00:43:11.720 --> 00:43:13.350
is a continuous process

00:43:13.350 --> 00:43:16.825
and it'll never end till the abolition

00:43:16.825 --> 00:43:18.543
of the wage form itself.

00:43:19.980 --> 00:43:24.980
So if SRT establishes the
non-capitalistic production

00:43:26.730 --> 00:43:28.810
of labor power and its reproduction

00:43:28.810 --> 00:43:32.885
through particular gendered
and racialized social relations

00:43:32.885 --> 00:43:35.651
it also raises more troubling questions

00:43:35.651 --> 00:43:39.163
about the procedures of its reproduction.

00:43:40.550 --> 00:43:43.470
The production of value and
the extraction of surplus value

00:43:43.470 --> 00:43:46.240
as demonstrated in capital,
have certain tendencies

00:43:46.240 --> 00:43:48.900
and counter tendencies
that seek equalization

00:43:48.900 --> 00:43:50.946
throughout the system.

00:43:50.946 --> 00:43:53.540
But the hallmark of capitalism,

00:43:53.540 --> 00:43:56.730
surely is it's deep social inequality

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:59.440
both between the capitalist class

00:43:59.440 --> 00:44:03.530
and workers within the classes themselves.

00:44:03.530 --> 00:44:07.670
So the production of surplus value unpacks

00:44:07.670 --> 00:44:11.330
the secret of inequalities
between capital and labor

00:44:11.330 --> 00:44:13.460
but what explains the sustenance

00:44:13.460 --> 00:44:16.163
of inequality amongst workers?

00:44:17.310 --> 00:44:19.483
What explains the wage gap?

00:44:20.520 --> 00:44:23.253
What explains the racialized wage gap?

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:27.360
So traditional Marxist
accounts of racism within

00:44:27.360 --> 00:44:30.470
the working class for
example, locate the production

00:44:30.470 --> 00:44:34.120
and reproduction race in
labor market competition.

00:44:34.120 --> 00:44:36.450
The argument goes that workers compete

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:39.520
with each other as individual
sellers of labor power

00:44:39.520 --> 00:44:42.376
on the market, and
historically dominant sections

00:44:42.376 --> 00:44:46.320
of the class compete with
the more vulnerable sections

00:44:46.320 --> 00:44:49.810
of the class over jobs, pensions
and social benefits, right?

00:44:49.810 --> 00:44:53.836
So all of the racism is
then reproduced or produced

00:44:53.836 --> 00:44:56.675
in the first place within
the labor market itself

00:44:56.675 --> 00:45:00.160
for competing jobs and so on.

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:04.120
So I agree with the broad
outlines of this account,

00:45:04.120 --> 00:45:07.356
but I think SRT urges
us to push the question

00:45:07.356 --> 00:45:11.190
of differentiation further

00:45:11.190 --> 00:45:14.914
and pitch it, not just at the
level of the labor market,

00:45:14.914 --> 00:45:19.914
but at the level of production
of the value of labor power.

00:45:20.473 --> 00:45:23.490
So the labor market is about the price

00:45:23.490 --> 00:45:27.190
of labor power, but we want
to situate the question

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:30.073
at the level of production
of the value of labor power.

00:45:31.570 --> 00:45:33.210
So the value of labor power

00:45:33.210 --> 00:45:35.610
as a commodity, we all
know, and this is again

00:45:35.610 --> 00:45:39.549
your first five chapters
of "Capital Volume I",

00:45:39.549 --> 00:45:42.300
like all commodities under capitalism,

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:44.210
the value of labor power is determined

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:46.340
by the value of the means of subsistence

00:45:48.060 --> 00:45:50.300
that is needed for it's production.

00:45:50.300 --> 00:45:51.673
So next slide, please.

00:45:53.740 --> 00:45:55.533
So this is your,

00:46:01.230 --> 00:46:03.930
sorry, Audrey, the next slide please.

00:46:03.930 --> 00:46:05.283
Oh, there we go, thank you.

00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:15.250
It is actually almost
institutionally recognized

00:46:15.250 --> 00:46:16.420
sometimes because that's what

00:46:16.420 --> 00:46:18.783
the federal minimum wage is, right?

00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:22.692
Forget the fact that
it's been stuck there,

00:46:22.692 --> 00:46:26.720
but the wage basket as it were,

00:46:26.720 --> 00:46:31.517
is your means of subsistence
that creates the labor power.

00:46:34.180 --> 00:46:35.830
But Marx reminds us

00:46:35.830 --> 00:46:39.210
that for this peculiar
commodity labor power

00:46:41.570 --> 00:46:43.550
and in contradistinction

00:46:43.550 --> 00:46:47.120
from all the other kinds of
commodities under capitalism

00:46:47.120 --> 00:46:50.060
there enters what Marx
calls, quote, unquote,

00:46:50.060 --> 00:46:53.222
an historical and moral element

00:46:53.222 --> 00:46:56.050
in the determination of its value.

00:46:56.050 --> 00:46:57.750
Now, what the hell does that mean?

00:46:58.790 --> 00:47:01.836
Because he says that there may be-

00:47:01.836 --> 00:47:06.836
- I'm sorry.
- That's okay.

00:47:07.763 --> 00:47:09.029
- [Woman] Sorry.

00:47:09.029 --> 00:47:10.196
- That's okay.

00:47:12.090 --> 00:47:14.790
So what is the historical
and moral element?

00:47:14.790 --> 00:47:19.790
So Marx introduces this
concept to say this

00:47:20.529 --> 00:47:22.728
that there might be a lower limit

00:47:22.728 --> 00:47:26.810
to what the laboring body needs
to reproduce itself, right?

00:47:26.810 --> 00:47:31.310
So there is only, you
can only like be hungry

00:47:31.310 --> 00:47:33.960
to a certain level or
thirsty to a certain level

00:47:33.960 --> 00:47:36.160
after which the human body will die.

00:47:36.160 --> 00:47:38.220
So there can be a lower limit

00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:42.583
to what the body needs to
go to work the next day.

00:47:44.250 --> 00:47:48.130
But there can be no fixed upper limit

00:47:48.130 --> 00:47:50.460
to the workers necessary needs,

00:47:50.460 --> 00:47:52.899
as needs themselves are dynamic,

00:47:52.899 --> 00:47:56.573
historically produced, and
shaped by the gains made

00:47:56.573 --> 00:47:59.340
by the working class through struggle.

00:47:59.340 --> 00:48:04.340
So a worker in the 1930s in Bangladesh,

00:48:06.094 --> 00:48:07.835
well, not in Bangladesh
at the time, India,

00:48:07.835 --> 00:48:12.835
has to be a worker in
1930s in British India

00:48:13.130 --> 00:48:18.130
will have very different
needs from the worker in 2020

00:48:19.380 --> 00:48:24.380
in India or Bangladesh, or
the worker in the US today.

00:48:25.355 --> 00:48:30.000
So because though what
we think we absolutely

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:32.970
need to go to work the next day

00:48:32.970 --> 00:48:36.540
what reproduces our labor power varies

00:48:36.540 --> 00:48:40.035
between countries between times.

00:48:40.035 --> 00:48:43.433
And so what Marx calls a dynamic process.

00:48:46.274 --> 00:48:48.300
So, in the number and extent of

00:48:48.300 --> 00:48:52.520
a workers necessary
wants, as Marx's confirms,

00:48:52.520 --> 00:48:56.390
are themselves the product of
historical development, right?

00:48:56.390 --> 00:49:00.670
So let's go to slide eight.

00:49:00.670 --> 00:49:05.670
So we can see in the next slide,

00:49:07.460 --> 00:49:08.733
some of the things,

00:49:16.331 --> 00:49:20.170
that we have come to accept as,

00:49:23.510 --> 00:49:25.253
sorry, the next slide please.

00:49:26.470 --> 00:49:30.460
So necessary wants we've come to accept

00:49:30.460 --> 00:49:32.520
as something that exists.

00:49:32.520 --> 00:49:35.680
And I want to talk about
the two figures here

00:49:35.680 --> 00:49:38.790
the correction and law
enforcement in a little bit,

00:49:38.790 --> 00:49:41.490
but this week we take for granted

00:49:41.490 --> 00:49:44.900
as some of the things today
that we take for granted

00:49:44.900 --> 00:49:47.069
as necessary wants that we have

00:49:47.069 --> 00:49:52.069
to reproduce as the workers.

00:49:53.960 --> 00:49:56.810
So if necessary wants
which determined the value

00:49:56.810 --> 00:49:59.630
of labor power are historical and dynamic,

00:49:59.630 --> 00:50:01.940
it follows then that different sections

00:50:01.940 --> 00:50:04.550
of the working class
produced at different moments

00:50:04.550 --> 00:50:06.650
of history will have different standards

00:50:06.650 --> 00:50:08.453
of necessary wants, right?

00:50:08.453 --> 00:50:11.720
The Irish worker for
Marx in direct contrast

00:50:11.720 --> 00:50:15.430
with her English counterpart embodied

00:50:15.430 --> 00:50:18.910
this production of
difference for Marx said

00:50:18.910 --> 00:50:21.837
that the Irish worker was, and I quote,

00:50:21.837 --> 00:50:25.030
"At that level of wage labor who accepted

00:50:25.030 --> 00:50:27.620
the most animal minimum of needs

00:50:27.620 --> 00:50:30.180
and subsistence in her
exchange with capital."

00:50:30.180 --> 00:50:31.620
Unquote.

00:50:31.620 --> 00:50:34.010
So the Irish worker accepted

00:50:35.977 --> 00:50:39.557
a much higher degree of exploitation than

00:50:39.557 --> 00:50:43.690
the English worker, according to Marx.

00:50:43.690 --> 00:50:46.540
So this version of productive difference

00:50:46.540 --> 00:50:51.080
then situates difference
in the very operation

00:50:51.080 --> 00:50:53.880
of labor powers reproduction, which means

00:50:53.880 --> 00:50:57.065
that labor power has
already been differentiated

00:50:57.065 --> 00:51:00.840
reproduced even before it reaches

00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:03.510
the field of labor market competition.

00:51:03.510 --> 00:51:07.880
Indeed, even the access or
entry into labor market cannot

00:51:07.880 --> 00:51:10.521
be a presupposition as such access

00:51:10.521 --> 00:51:12.970
and entry across different sections

00:51:12.970 --> 00:51:15.883
of the class exists precisely due

00:51:17.546 --> 00:51:19.937
to differential reproduction.

00:51:19.937 --> 00:51:21.783
So now let's go back

00:51:21.783 --> 00:51:26.783
to the correction and and
the police, law enforcement,

00:51:27.340 --> 00:51:29.780
actually these two categories

00:51:29.780 --> 00:51:32.110
in any working class community has

00:51:32.110 --> 00:51:36.396
a very differential effect on
black workers, Latino workers,

00:51:36.396 --> 00:51:40.460
than they have on white workers.

00:51:40.460 --> 00:51:41.936
The kind of disciplining

00:51:41.936 --> 00:51:44.730
categories that exist have

00:51:44.730 --> 00:51:47.870
very different kind of expectations

00:51:47.870 --> 00:51:51.130
and notion of necessary wants

00:51:51.130 --> 00:51:54.263
than between black and white
workers or Latino workers.

00:51:55.710 --> 00:51:57.660
So attention to social reproductive

00:51:57.660 --> 00:52:01.760
activities then reveal the
specific social processes

00:52:01.760 --> 00:52:05.990
through which certain workers
embody certain qualities

00:52:05.990 --> 00:52:09.550
of labor power arrive at
the doorstep of capital,

00:52:09.550 --> 00:52:13.560
more vulnerable and degraded than others.

00:52:13.560 --> 00:52:18.540
Ruthlessly depressed
levels of necessary wants

00:52:18.540 --> 00:52:21.920
for Latino workers in
the US is one example

00:52:21.920 --> 00:52:25.630
of brutally differentiated
processes of social reproduction

00:52:25.630 --> 00:52:29.610
between migrant workers and
their citizen counterparts.

00:52:29.610 --> 00:52:32.160
The median space per person

00:52:32.160 --> 00:52:37.160
in a Latino household in
the US is 350 square feet.

00:52:37.399 --> 00:52:41.990
80 square feet less than than that

00:52:41.990 --> 00:52:44.850
of the average non migrant family living

00:52:44.850 --> 00:52:46.173
below the poverty line.

00:52:47.274 --> 00:52:51.036
So in a similar vein, I don't need to go

00:52:51.036 --> 00:52:53.960
through all of this,
but if you, if you look

00:52:53.960 --> 00:52:58.960
at how COVID-19 has affected
black working class communities

00:53:03.590 --> 00:53:06.050
and Latino working class
communities compared

00:53:06.050 --> 00:53:08.260
to white working class communities

00:53:08.260 --> 00:53:12.840
it is very clear that previous processes

00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:14.550
of social differentiation,

00:53:14.550 --> 00:53:18.193
redlining, environmental
racism had already

00:53:18.193 --> 00:53:21.455
made these communities
far more vulnerable.

00:53:21.455 --> 00:53:24.850
So when the disease finally hit

00:53:24.850 --> 00:53:27.491
it obviously had a differential response

00:53:27.491 --> 00:53:29.060
in these communities.

00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:31.870
As well as, not just those kinds

00:53:31.870 --> 00:53:34.630
of redlining and environmental racism,

00:53:34.630 --> 00:53:39.630
but where you know, factories
producing environmentally

00:53:41.733 --> 00:53:46.733
degrading products are
cited far more frequently

00:53:47.243 --> 00:53:49.390
in black communities than they are

00:53:49.390 --> 00:53:50.910
in non-black communities, right?

00:53:50.910 --> 00:53:52.227
So this has been established.

00:53:52.227 --> 00:53:53.660
"The New York Times" has actually

00:53:53.660 --> 00:53:55.970
done pretty decent reporting

00:53:55.970 --> 00:54:00.810
on redlining and industrial
waste in recent times.

00:54:00.810 --> 00:54:04.350
So we are looking at then

00:54:04.350 --> 00:54:08.540
the factor that certain communities

00:54:08.540 --> 00:54:13.090
are historically reproduced
to be more vulnerable even

00:54:13.090 --> 00:54:14.990
before they hit the labor market.

00:54:14.990 --> 00:54:18.770
So the idea that the
differentiation happens

00:54:18.770 --> 00:54:22.880
in the labor market, then
we need to sort of revise

00:54:22.880 --> 00:54:26.150
and look at these veins of
social reproduction that exists

00:54:26.150 --> 00:54:31.150
before the worker gets to
the door of the labor market.

00:54:31.740 --> 00:54:36.450
So access to housing, police
violence, substandard schools

00:54:36.450 --> 00:54:38.440
and healthcare are obvious

00:54:38.440 --> 00:54:41.880
ingredients that determine
the level of necessary wants

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:45.430
for a section of the working
class and thereby determined

00:54:45.430 --> 00:54:48.770
and lower the value of their labor power.

00:54:48.770 --> 00:54:50.210
But the lowering of value

00:54:50.210 --> 00:54:54.020
for one section of workers
always has a conditioning impulse

00:54:54.020 --> 00:54:56.490
for all sections for lower wages

00:54:56.490 --> 00:54:59.790
for some workers can allow
capital to rationalize

00:54:59.790 --> 00:55:01.810
and lower wages for all workers.

00:55:01.810 --> 00:55:04.577
So degraded, social reproduction
of racialized workers

00:55:04.577 --> 00:55:09.203
thus can help establish a regime
of cheapened wages for all.

00:55:10.540 --> 00:55:13.450
So I want to conclude by circling back

00:55:13.450 --> 00:55:17.740
to the morphology of labor
power, as within it is fused

00:55:17.740 --> 00:55:20.010
the argument offered in "Capital"

00:55:20.010 --> 00:55:22.640
and the significance of SRT.

00:55:22.640 --> 00:55:25.276
So while it is true, that capitalism tries

00:55:25.276 --> 00:55:27.977
to produce the working class it needs,

00:55:27.977 --> 00:55:32.300
"Capital Volume I" shows
us that workers struggle

00:55:32.300 --> 00:55:35.860
against the wage form is a necessary part

00:55:35.860 --> 00:55:40.060
of the internal dynamics of the
system and its reproduction.

00:55:40.060 --> 00:55:42.881
The worker will always tend
towards the development

00:55:42.881 --> 00:55:46.670
of her own needs through
life-making activities.

00:55:46.670 --> 00:55:49.490
While capital will continually tend

00:55:49.490 --> 00:55:51.362
towards limiting such activities

00:55:51.362 --> 00:55:54.340
to increase its share of surplus value,

00:55:54.340 --> 00:55:56.380
and hence civilization.

00:55:56.380 --> 00:56:00.500
Collective organizing on the
basis of solidarity remains

00:56:00.500 --> 00:56:02.740
the only way the working class can

00:56:02.740 --> 00:56:07.520
win this unequally resourced
battle against capital.

00:56:07.520 --> 00:56:10.730
The warning we received from SRT, however,

00:56:10.730 --> 00:56:14.220
is that a multi-racial
working class unity will

00:56:14.220 --> 00:56:19.220
not arise spontaneously through
either left-wing propaganda

00:56:19.473 --> 00:56:22.411
or volunteerism for the differentiation

00:56:22.411 --> 00:56:26.170
of the working class is
produced and sustained

00:56:26.170 --> 00:56:29.040
at a cellular level of the system.

00:56:29.040 --> 00:56:32.507
It is clear that vicious border policies,

00:56:32.507 --> 00:56:35.986
gendered and racialized
labor regimes are only some

00:56:35.986 --> 00:56:39.780
of the ways in which capitalism seeks

00:56:39.780 --> 00:56:42.130
to globally reproduce the working class

00:56:42.130 --> 00:56:46.100
in combined, but deeply unequal ways.

00:56:46.100 --> 00:56:47.620
Against this warning,

00:56:47.620 --> 00:56:52.560
the aspiration we received
from SRT is equally important,

00:56:52.560 --> 00:56:55.704
as a unitary theory,
revealing the relationship

00:56:55.704 --> 00:56:58.430
between point of production of commodities

00:56:58.430 --> 00:57:01.390
and the spaces of
reproduction of labor power,

00:57:01.390 --> 00:57:06.357
SRT imbues every struggle for
enhanced social reproduction

00:57:06.357 --> 00:57:09.370
with anticapitalist possibilities.

00:57:09.370 --> 00:57:12.620
A working class movement that will be able

00:57:12.620 --> 00:57:16.520
to give form to such
possibilities cannot simply

00:57:16.520 --> 00:57:19.810
retrace older cultures of solidarity

00:57:19.810 --> 00:57:22.980
but it will have to
actively forge new wants.

00:57:22.980 --> 00:57:27.160
Such a movement must champion
with equal vigor working

00:57:27.160 --> 00:57:29.750
class struggles based in communities

00:57:29.750 --> 00:57:34.180
around water, around police
violence, around housing,

00:57:34.180 --> 00:57:36.460
alongside workplace struggles.

00:57:36.460 --> 00:57:38.480
It will have to learn to articulate

00:57:38.480 --> 00:57:40.690
demands on a double register,

00:57:40.690 --> 00:57:43.230
simultaneously appealing to

00:57:43.230 --> 00:57:45.240
the universal and the specific.

00:57:45.240 --> 00:57:48.575
We should simultaneously
demand universal health care

00:57:48.575 --> 00:57:51.332
but make specific demands for access

00:57:51.332 --> 00:57:52.970
to reproductive care,

00:57:52.970 --> 00:57:57.576
for better healthcare for
black people and so forth.

00:57:57.576 --> 00:57:59.767
These should not be understood as

00:57:59.767 --> 00:58:02.210
two counter posed strategies,

00:58:02.210 --> 00:58:05.703
but as to moments in a
single necessary process.

00:58:06.570 --> 00:58:08.040
Next slide please.

00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:10.965
So if "Capital Volume I" remains

00:58:10.965 --> 00:58:13.180
one of the most savage critiques

00:58:13.180 --> 00:58:15.580
of capitalism as a system,

00:58:15.580 --> 00:58:18.380
SRT, or Social Reproduction Theory,

00:58:18.380 --> 00:58:21.640
can perhaps be thought of as animating

00:58:21.640 --> 00:58:26.560
that critique with embodied
voices of workers in struggle.

00:58:26.560 --> 00:58:27.393
Thank you.

00:58:30.360 --> 00:58:31.193
- Thank you very much.

00:58:31.193 --> 00:58:33.000
This is the hole that
we fall into at the end

00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:36.628
of every talk, where there
would be thunderous applause.

00:58:36.628 --> 00:58:39.191
I know that that's happening

00:58:39.191 --> 00:58:44.191
and that there's also feelings
of a possible new solidarity.

00:58:47.110 --> 00:58:48.815
So thank you for the talk.

00:58:48.815 --> 00:58:49.920
I want to kick

00:58:49.920 --> 00:58:53.180
off with a question and then
we can start asking folks

00:58:53.180 --> 00:58:54.780
if they want to put questions

00:58:54.780 --> 00:58:57.733
in the chat and we will
take them in order.

00:59:00.051 --> 00:59:02.453
I want to ask you this question.

00:59:04.900 --> 00:59:06.900
So I've thought for a long time

00:59:06.900 --> 00:59:11.730
that the division of labor is
one of the fundamental motors

00:59:11.730 --> 00:59:15.540
of "Capital Volume I", one and
of capital as Marx sees it.

00:59:15.540 --> 00:59:17.170
That's not just a static thing

00:59:17.170 --> 00:59:21.047
or an advantage to capital
that labor be divided,

00:59:21.047 --> 00:59:23.030
but it's a kind of dynamic motor

00:59:23.030 --> 00:59:25.500
which keeps getting divided and redivided

00:59:26.371 --> 00:59:31.371
for the purpose of
increasing accumulation.

00:59:33.630 --> 00:59:37.510
And I really liked that
the reproductive space

00:59:37.510 --> 00:59:40.650
is brought into this by SRT,

00:59:40.650 --> 00:59:42.990
it was always there,

00:59:42.990 --> 00:59:45.830
but it's brought into this as an effect

00:59:45.830 --> 00:59:48.910
of the division of labor,
part of the division

00:59:48.910 --> 00:59:50.861
of labor almost as though
there's a continuum

00:59:50.861 --> 00:59:54.260
and that the production
floor is right next

00:59:54.260 --> 00:59:56.630
to the reproduction floor.

00:59:56.630 --> 00:59:59.743
And these forces are
working on both of them.

01:00:01.010 --> 01:00:02.283
I guess I want to ask,

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:09.510
if you see them still as,

01:00:09.510 --> 01:00:11.910
or if you see them as
qualitatively different,

01:00:11.910 --> 01:00:15.000
these two moments?

01:00:15.000 --> 01:00:18.850
If we should consider them both labor

01:00:18.850 --> 01:00:20.450
in the same way,

01:00:20.450 --> 01:00:23.460
if putting them on a continuum makes

01:00:23.460 --> 01:00:26.500
a stronger possibility for solidarity.

01:00:26.500 --> 01:00:28.090
And if there have been examples

01:00:28.090 --> 01:00:32.110
in history where let's
say the domestic sphere

01:00:32.110 --> 01:00:34.470
and the production sphere have
gone out on strike together

01:00:34.470 --> 01:00:37.483
you showed two images and
the woman is split in half,

01:00:38.865 --> 01:00:40.590
but are there examples that you know of

01:00:40.590 --> 01:00:43.223
where the solidarity has happened?

01:00:44.860 --> 01:00:46.217
- Great question, Paul.

01:00:46.217 --> 01:00:51.217
So first of all the division is

01:00:52.274 --> 01:00:56.680
in a sense when we're talking about it,

01:00:56.680 --> 01:00:58.430
we're talking about it

01:01:02.797 --> 01:01:06.340
to make it easy to analyze, right?

01:01:06.340 --> 01:01:08.580
So the division is the
theoretical division,

01:01:08.580 --> 01:01:10.920
but in reality, you know,

01:01:10.920 --> 01:01:15.450
the process has to happen
simultaneously, right?

01:01:15.450 --> 01:01:18.717
So it's a conceptual
division that we've created

01:01:18.717 --> 01:01:21.703
in order to understand the process.

01:01:23.336 --> 01:01:27.870
So let me start by saying
how it's different rather

01:01:27.870 --> 01:01:30.130
than how it's same, because
I think we've talked

01:01:30.130 --> 01:01:33.290
about how it's simultaneous
and how it's same.

01:01:33.290 --> 01:01:35.240
So let's think about that.

01:01:35.240 --> 01:01:40.240
So first of all, domestic
labor while completely

01:01:40.938 --> 01:01:45.430
the precondition if you
like to wage labor, right?

01:01:45.430 --> 01:01:47.960
So if there was no domestic labor

01:01:47.960 --> 01:01:49.610
there would be no wage labor.

01:01:49.610 --> 01:01:52.210
So while it's a precondition to wage labor

01:01:52.210 --> 01:01:55.070
however domestic labor
does not produce value.

01:01:55.070 --> 01:01:59.960
So we could not have equalized time

01:01:59.960 --> 01:02:04.560
for diaper change across
all household units

01:02:04.560 --> 01:02:06.623
of a social formation,

01:02:07.896 --> 01:02:11.400
and thereby produce value from it.

01:02:11.400 --> 01:02:13.000
So it doesn't produce value

01:02:13.000 --> 01:02:17.972
in that sense, even though it
is actually the precondition

01:02:17.972 --> 01:02:19.700
to the production value.

01:02:19.700 --> 01:02:22.240
So that's one for sure.

01:02:22.240 --> 01:02:24.640
We cannot have equalization across units.

01:02:24.640 --> 01:02:26.270
So we cannot have abstract labor

01:02:26.270 --> 01:02:28.169
in the domestic theory,

01:02:28.169 --> 01:02:30.420
it has to be by necessity concrete labor.

01:02:30.420 --> 01:02:33.160
So that's one.

01:02:33.160 --> 01:02:36.140
And the second thing is that

01:02:37.350 --> 01:02:40.120
I've said that capital does not have

01:02:40.120 --> 01:02:44.453
full control over this sphere,

01:02:45.679 --> 01:02:48.710
but often when I've
talked about it this way

01:02:48.710 --> 01:02:50.310
it may be misunderstood

01:02:50.310 --> 01:02:53.094
sometimes by saying that it

01:02:53.094 --> 01:02:55.420
lies outside of the circuit
of value production.

01:02:55.420 --> 01:02:56.500
Well, it doesn't.

01:02:56.500 --> 01:02:59.075
So what I mean is it doesn't fall

01:02:59.075 --> 01:03:01.720
within the direct control of capital.

01:03:01.720 --> 01:03:04.713
It doesn't mean it lies
outside of the circle.

01:03:05.774 --> 01:03:08.940
A very simple example would be you working

01:03:11.050 --> 01:03:13.200
in your garden or one working

01:03:13.200 --> 01:03:17.880
in their garden is a
non-value producing work.

01:03:17.880 --> 01:03:19.480
It's a life-making activity.

01:03:19.480 --> 01:03:20.960
It's a concrete labor.

01:03:20.960 --> 01:03:22.453
It's a beautiful thing.

01:03:23.410 --> 01:03:25.580
However, you can only work

01:03:25.580 --> 01:03:30.580
in your garden when your wage
labor schedule allows you to

01:03:31.065 --> 01:03:35.020
so you can't simply work in
your garden when you want to.

01:03:35.020 --> 01:03:39.007
So the way that work is different,

01:03:40.870 --> 01:03:45.010
the way that work is
done is actually shaped

01:03:45.010 --> 01:03:47.700
by the impulses of wage work.

01:03:47.700 --> 01:03:49.860
So even though it is not.

01:03:49.860 --> 01:03:54.860
But again, because it's
human concrete labor

01:03:55.290 --> 01:03:59.700
it constantly has these
potentials for disruption.

01:03:59.700 --> 01:04:02.550
And obviously wage labor does that too.

01:04:02.550 --> 01:04:05.760
That's what a strike is,
but we should also think

01:04:05.760 --> 01:04:09.410
about the moments of
disruption that can happen here

01:04:09.410 --> 01:04:11.633
because capital doesn't
have direct control.

01:04:12.740 --> 01:04:15.531
To come to the more
important question is when

01:04:15.531 --> 01:04:20.223
have they ever struck work simultaneously?

01:04:24.070 --> 01:04:28.240
Let's remember that the two
most important revolutions

01:04:29.610 --> 01:04:33.540
of modern times, the
French and the Russians,

01:04:33.540 --> 01:04:38.540
1789 and 1917, both started
off with bread rights,

01:04:41.170 --> 01:04:43.640
which was, you know, women.

01:04:43.640 --> 01:04:45.700
and this is acknowledged in all

01:04:45.700 --> 01:04:47.720
of the contemporary writing

01:04:47.720 --> 01:04:52.720
that it was women breaking
through Baker's shops

01:04:53.420 --> 01:04:55.343
where bread was being horded,

01:04:56.598 --> 01:05:01.230
or looting shops and
demanding bread for themselves

01:05:01.230 --> 01:05:04.120
and their families that
actually sparked off

01:05:04.120 --> 01:05:07.744
the revolution which set off
then a series of wage strikes.

01:05:07.744 --> 01:05:10.710
So it, so the question

01:05:10.710 --> 01:05:15.300
for us as social justice
activists should be

01:05:17.660 --> 01:05:22.660
to consider not so much that
these two spheres are separate

01:05:24.290 --> 01:05:29.290
but to consider that sometimes
a resistance will spark

01:05:29.300 --> 01:05:33.273
in one sphere, but can easily
spread to the other, right.

01:05:34.660 --> 01:05:35.760
- That's very helpful

01:05:35.760 --> 01:05:40.050
while we're waiting for
people to post some questions.

01:05:40.050 --> 01:05:42.740
If they have them, maybe I'll follow up.

01:05:42.740 --> 01:05:45.720
I wonder if you could say this is more

01:05:45.720 --> 01:05:49.090
of a technical issue in in the dialectic,

01:05:49.090 --> 01:05:51.250
but I wonder if you
could say a little more

01:05:51.250 --> 01:05:52.889
about how the domestic sphere,

01:05:52.889 --> 01:05:55.933
the reproductive sphere is included.

01:05:57.020 --> 01:05:58.570
So for example, I can see it

01:05:58.570 --> 01:06:01.370
as a possible point of resistance,

01:06:01.370 --> 01:06:04.970
because there's just
other values or purposes,

01:06:04.970 --> 01:06:07.420
you call them life-making, right?

01:06:07.420 --> 01:06:09.650
And there's remnants of,

01:06:09.650 --> 01:06:12.480
not just remnants, but
full-fledged indorsement

01:06:12.480 --> 01:06:16.390
of other hermeneutic
frameworks like religious ones

01:06:16.390 --> 01:06:21.360
or whatever it be
outside of the workplace.

01:06:21.360 --> 01:06:24.360
And even inside of the workplace
that go along alongside it.

01:06:25.210 --> 01:06:27.841
So you could say, well,
life-making is a separate value

01:06:27.841 --> 01:06:30.220
and it can offer a point

01:06:30.220 --> 01:06:34.883
of resistance on its terms at times.

01:06:36.120 --> 01:06:38.158
But there's also a pressure

01:06:38.158 --> 01:06:40.303
from capital, let's say,

01:06:42.219 --> 01:06:45.390
to reduce that life-making activity

01:06:45.390 --> 01:06:48.401
to the absolute minimum because

01:06:48.401 --> 01:06:50.517
even though it doesn't contribute to value

01:06:50.517 --> 01:06:53.580
or you could say you can't generalize it.

01:06:53.580 --> 01:06:57.000
So you can't measure
value on diaper changes.

01:06:57.000 --> 01:06:59.903
That's a really useful way to put it.

01:07:02.570 --> 01:07:05.830
The worker has to let's
say cover in the value

01:07:05.830 --> 01:07:09.560
of their labor, not only
herself, but her dependence.

01:07:09.560 --> 01:07:12.150
So it's contributing in some
way to the value of labor.

01:07:12.150 --> 01:07:14.657
Like the magnitude of the
value of labor is also

01:07:14.657 --> 01:07:19.657
how much this heteronormative
family requires to go on.

01:07:20.990 --> 01:07:22.630
So if capital could get rid of it,

01:07:22.630 --> 01:07:25.453
I guess it would, no?

01:07:26.930 --> 01:07:28.670
- Get rid of the family?

01:07:28.670 --> 01:07:30.090
- Domestic sphere,

01:07:30.090 --> 01:07:32.270
reproductive labor, as much as possible.

01:07:32.270 --> 01:07:34.050
I'm thinking of the other side,

01:07:34.050 --> 01:07:39.050
which is not resistance
from those other positions,

01:07:39.770 --> 01:07:43.363
but the interest would be kind of like,

01:07:46.706 --> 01:07:48.123
to get rid of it.

01:07:50.800 --> 01:07:52.550
So it doesn't seem to be possible

01:07:52.550 --> 01:07:53.780
but I wonder if you see pressures

01:07:53.780 --> 01:07:58.193
from that side also, whatever
that means, automation.

01:08:00.311 --> 01:08:05.311
- So to get rid of it,
because it wants more time

01:08:08.149 --> 01:08:11.840
in the wage sphere, is
that what you're saying?

01:08:11.840 --> 01:08:15.060
- It would theoretically
reduce the value of labor

01:08:15.060 --> 01:08:16.880
if labor didn't also have to provide

01:08:16.880 --> 01:08:20.653
for the lives of the
laborers dependence, right?

01:08:22.520 --> 01:08:23.783
- Sure. Yeah.

01:08:25.160 --> 01:08:29.050
I mean, let's think about
the historical examples here

01:08:29.050 --> 01:08:32.020
that's a really, really great point,

01:08:32.020 --> 01:08:36.440
actually, Paul and I'm
very grateful for it

01:08:36.440 --> 01:08:38.630
because we should have
talked about it a little bit.

01:08:38.630 --> 01:08:41.130
Like let's think about
the historical origins

01:08:41.130 --> 01:08:43.714
of capitalism as a historic system in,

01:08:43.714 --> 01:08:47.160
let's say, Northern England, right?

01:08:47.160 --> 01:08:52.160
So, I mean, this is where
Marx in volume one talks

01:08:55.531 --> 01:09:00.410
so laudably about the
factory inspectors, right?

01:09:00.410 --> 01:09:03.030
Because what capital was actually doing

01:09:03.030 --> 01:09:05.400
was pulling all of the labor

01:09:06.270 --> 01:09:08.750
into the factory and therefore

01:09:08.750 --> 01:09:11.720
thereby completely undermining
social reproduction

01:09:11.720 --> 01:09:13.100
of the working class family.

01:09:13.100 --> 01:09:14.510
So people were just dying.

01:09:14.510 --> 01:09:18.870
And I always say, when I teach
"Capital Volume I", one is

01:09:18.870 --> 01:09:22.770
that the companion piece is
not necessarily, excuse me.

01:09:25.186 --> 01:09:28.617
but the companion piece
is actually Angels'

01:09:28.617 --> 01:09:31.450
condition of the working
class in England, right?

01:09:31.450 --> 01:09:34.581
Because that shows you in very

01:09:34.581 --> 01:09:39.581
descriptive ways, what happens if a

01:09:39.636 --> 01:09:44.360
social reproduction capital does not kind

01:09:44.360 --> 01:09:47.470
of close it's eyes to that loss of profit

01:09:47.470 --> 01:09:49.250
by maintaining the working-class family?

01:09:49.250 --> 01:09:50.500
What happens?

01:09:50.500 --> 01:09:55.500
It undermines its very
possibility of existence, right?

01:09:55.790 --> 01:09:57.680
Because without the working class family

01:09:57.680 --> 01:10:00.759
without the life-making,
there would be no workers.

01:10:00.759 --> 01:10:05.590
So this is in a way something that we have

01:10:05.590 --> 01:10:08.080
to actually think about really

01:10:08.080 --> 01:10:09.440
and this is why I'm so grateful

01:10:09.440 --> 01:10:10.900
for the point you bring up,

01:10:10.900 --> 01:10:13.210
we have to think about this very clearly

01:10:13.210 --> 01:10:18.173
in terms of the current
ecological emergency

01:10:19.080 --> 01:10:24.080
because accumulation is actually pushing,

01:10:24.470 --> 01:10:28.197
not so much in terms of

01:10:28.197 --> 01:10:31.651
the 18th century or early 19th century

01:10:31.651 --> 01:10:35.530
sort of the extinguishing
of the working class family.

01:10:35.530 --> 01:10:38.687
But it is pushing again

01:10:38.687 --> 01:10:41.717
to a point where it's undermining

01:10:41.717 --> 01:10:43.940
even the possibility of value

01:10:43.940 --> 01:10:47.340
production and accumulation,
by pushing the planet

01:10:47.340 --> 01:10:51.060
to the edge of climate catastrophe

01:10:51.060 --> 01:10:53.780
which will undermine its own
condition of possibility.

01:10:53.780 --> 01:10:58.350
So I think this is the
dynamic that we have

01:10:58.350 --> 01:11:01.620
to be attentive to
constantly under capitalism,

01:11:01.620 --> 01:11:05.556
that what it can get away with

01:11:05.556 --> 01:11:10.556
by not giving and what it can,

01:11:10.660 --> 01:11:12.550
what it is forced to give,

01:11:12.550 --> 01:11:16.760
albeit reluctantly in order
to ensure this continuous

01:11:16.760 --> 01:11:19.673
reliable production, reproduction circuit.

01:11:20.880 --> 01:11:22.070
- That's very helpful. Thank you.

01:11:22.070 --> 01:11:25.060
And I love the picture of
capital, the capital system

01:11:25.060 --> 01:11:27.706
as being this kind of
anxious little person

01:11:27.706 --> 01:11:30.513
worried about the incursions on all sides.

01:11:31.530 --> 01:11:33.880
We have some questions and I want to start

01:11:33.880 --> 01:11:38.670
with a question by Elena
Gomes if we can unmute Elena.

01:11:38.670 --> 01:11:40.770
And if you don't mind asking your question

01:11:43.170 --> 01:11:45.180
- Hi, thank you so much.

01:11:45.180 --> 01:11:48.470
I was just wondering if
you could talk a little bit

01:11:48.470 --> 01:11:52.520
about how you see the
kind of historicization

01:11:52.520 --> 01:11:55.590
of SRT in relation to Marxist feminism,

01:11:55.590 --> 01:11:57.960
particularly in the 1970s,

01:11:57.960 --> 01:12:00.650
I'm interested in kind
of whether you see this

01:12:00.650 --> 01:12:04.660
as like a theoretical departure
or if it's an extension or

01:12:04.660 --> 01:12:09.660
if it's kind of out of a different
tradition and whether SRT

01:12:10.450 --> 01:12:13.863
is able to ask questions
that were maybe not available

01:12:13.863 --> 01:12:16.830
to the theorists such as Selma James

01:12:16.830 --> 01:12:19.043
and Leopoldina Fortunati, et cetera?

01:12:23.010 --> 01:12:26.010
- Shall I just answer or wait for more?

01:12:26.010 --> 01:12:27.350
- Would you like to gather a few

01:12:27.350 --> 01:12:28.744
or do you want to answer one by one?

01:12:28.744 --> 01:12:29.990
It's up to you.

01:12:29.990 --> 01:12:31.460
- Let me just try to answer this

01:12:31.460 --> 01:12:34.420
because I think it's a very important one.

01:12:34.420 --> 01:12:37.183
So thank you for that, Elena.

01:12:38.463 --> 01:12:43.360
So I think first of all

01:12:43.360 --> 01:12:46.740
we should start any discussion about 1970s

01:12:47.850 --> 01:12:52.543
Marxist feminism by
saying that this version

01:12:54.140 --> 01:12:58.620
of SRT, as we develop
from "Capital Volume I"

01:12:58.620 --> 01:13:00.313
in more recent times,

01:13:02.779 --> 01:13:03.750
and just to go back,

01:13:03.750 --> 01:13:08.750
Lisa Vogel, who kind of
pioneered some of this thinking,

01:13:09.410 --> 01:13:12.060
Lisa's book was actually
published in the early eighties.

01:13:12.060 --> 01:13:16.280
So even though it has had a sort

01:13:16.280 --> 01:13:19.671
of regeneration in recent times

01:13:19.671 --> 01:13:23.700
and we can talk about why
that regeneration has happened

01:13:23.700 --> 01:13:26.128
because I have some ideas about that,

01:13:26.128 --> 01:13:30.827
Lisa's work actually
isn't too sort of modern,

01:13:30.827 --> 01:13:32.380
"Marxism and the Oppression of Women"

01:13:32.380 --> 01:13:35.210
was first published in 1983.

01:13:35.210 --> 01:13:40.210
So, but it is actually,
it forms a departure

01:13:40.960 --> 01:13:44.480
from the earlier Marxist feminist thinking

01:13:44.480 --> 01:13:46.056
on domestic labor.

01:13:46.056 --> 01:13:49.578
Because SRT, as we talked about,

01:13:49.578 --> 01:13:52.370
we talk a lot about domestic labor

01:13:52.370 --> 01:13:56.890
but it is a very different
kind of placing and positioning

01:13:56.890 --> 01:13:59.930
and theorizing and
exploring of domestic labor,

01:13:59.930 --> 01:14:03.450
very different from Selma James

01:14:03.450 --> 01:14:06.217
and Sylvia Federici and
Leopoldina Fortunati.

01:14:09.200 --> 01:14:12.740
So the first thing to say is

01:14:12.740 --> 01:14:16.920
I think I owe a debt of
gratitude, a big debt

01:14:16.920 --> 01:14:21.920
of gratitude to the 1970s
domestic labor debate.

01:14:23.790 --> 01:14:28.129
And the reason for that
is remember domestic labor

01:14:28.129 --> 01:14:33.129
as a theoretical category lies,

01:14:33.530 --> 01:14:36.530
the seeds of it lie in "Capital Volume I".

01:14:36.530 --> 01:14:39.010
The discussion of it happens

01:14:39.010 --> 01:14:42.174
in early Marxist and socialist thought

01:14:42.174 --> 01:14:45.440
one can think about Alexandra Kollontai.

01:14:45.440 --> 01:14:50.217
But it's kind of that discussion

01:14:51.358 --> 01:14:54.640
is submerged and lost in history.

01:14:54.640 --> 01:14:57.680
And it is these comrades
and activists and scholar

01:14:57.680 --> 01:15:02.190
activists of the 1970s who
resurrect this category

01:15:02.190 --> 01:15:05.830
but politicize it and theorize it.

01:15:05.830 --> 01:15:09.760
So we all actually will owe
a real debt of gratitude

01:15:09.760 --> 01:15:12.700
to these thinkers and activists.

01:15:12.700 --> 01:15:14.460
And they don't just theorize it.

01:15:14.460 --> 01:15:17.010
They actually go to battle on it.

01:15:17.010 --> 01:15:19.343
Which is most, most important.

01:15:20.290 --> 01:15:21.910
So having said that,

01:15:21.910 --> 01:15:25.000
I also have to say, as
you might've guessed,

01:15:25.000 --> 01:15:30.000
as I indicated that I
don't think I don't agree

01:15:30.090 --> 01:15:32.390
with the way they theorize domestic labor

01:15:32.390 --> 01:15:35.020
because these theorists argue

01:15:35.020 --> 01:15:37.430
that domestic labor
actually creates value.

01:15:37.430 --> 01:15:42.010
and I don't think it does,

01:15:42.010 --> 01:15:43.670
it is the precondition for value,

01:15:43.670 --> 01:15:45.460
but it doesn't actually create value.

01:15:45.460 --> 01:15:49.420
So that's a sort of
major difference we have

01:15:49.420 --> 01:15:51.963
in the way it is theorized.

01:15:56.408 --> 01:15:59.430
And because theorized slightly differently

01:15:59.430 --> 01:16:04.430
it often can have different
strategic conclusions, right?

01:16:04.600 --> 01:16:09.600
So if we realize that domestic
labor can create value,

01:16:10.960 --> 01:16:15.570
then we can think about
ways in which striking

01:16:17.380 --> 01:16:19.860
or demanding wages for domestic labor

01:16:19.860 --> 01:16:22.400
is actually going to disrupt capital.

01:16:22.400 --> 01:16:25.883
And we have to and again,

01:16:31.280 --> 01:16:34.387
if there is a strike
to stop domestic labor

01:16:34.387 --> 01:16:37.200
as the women's strikes have shown

01:16:37.200 --> 01:16:42.200
then that is actually a very
powerful means to do that.

01:16:42.540 --> 01:16:45.240
But we also have to think about the ways

01:16:45.240 --> 01:16:50.240
in which wage labor has
a conditioning impulse

01:16:50.240 --> 01:16:52.080
on domestic labor.

01:16:52.080 --> 01:16:56.751
And so to separate those two in my mind is

01:16:56.751 --> 01:16:59.510
is not gonna be helpful,

01:16:59.510 --> 01:17:04.510
but I want to end with saying
that if there is a barricade,

01:17:05.000 --> 01:17:07.590
then I think all of us are on

01:17:07.590 --> 01:17:10.490
the same side of that barricade.

01:17:10.490 --> 01:17:13.400
Vis-a-vis how we proceed
in the feminist struggle.

01:17:13.400 --> 01:17:15.630
And I think these discussions

01:17:15.630 --> 01:17:19.210
and debates and differences
are actually very productive

01:17:19.210 --> 01:17:21.700
in which ways of moving forward.

01:17:24.370 --> 01:17:28.950
- There's a healthy roll of
questions in in the chat.

01:17:28.950 --> 01:17:31.500
So I'm going to read, do
you want to take them one

01:17:31.500 --> 01:17:34.093
by one or do you prefer grouping?

01:17:34.093 --> 01:17:34.926
- It's okay.

01:17:34.926 --> 01:17:35.759
Whichever.

01:17:35.759 --> 01:17:36.890
- So the next one I'm gonna read

01:17:36.890 --> 01:17:39.940
out from Vanessa Gubbins
following up on that one.

01:17:39.940 --> 01:17:41.270
Could you say something more

01:17:41.270 --> 01:17:44.400
about the potential of
anticapitalist charge

01:17:44.400 --> 01:17:46.730
in life-making activities in relation

01:17:46.730 --> 01:17:49.823
to the difficulties that can
arise due to real subsumption?

01:17:54.205 --> 01:17:56.233
- Real subsumption,

01:17:59.909 --> 01:18:04.800
could you talk about it a little more?

01:18:04.800 --> 01:18:07.200
- Vanessa, do you want to hop on?

01:18:07.200 --> 01:18:12.200
- Just tell me how you
relate sort of domestic labor

01:18:13.510 --> 01:18:16.510
or life-making activity
to real subsumption.

01:18:16.510 --> 01:18:18.030
What did you mean by that?

01:18:27.546 --> 01:18:31.713
- We're just unmuting and
getting ourselves ready.

01:18:34.900 --> 01:18:37.403
Audrey, can you unmute Vanessa?

01:18:40.410 --> 01:18:41.490
- [Vanessa] Can you hear me?

01:18:41.490 --> 01:18:42.620
- [Paul] Yes, we have you now.

01:18:42.620 --> 01:18:44.250
- [Vanessa] Hi, sorry about that.

01:18:44.250 --> 01:18:47.570
Thank you so much for
such an incredible talk.

01:18:47.570 --> 01:18:49.850
I actually think you already answered

01:18:49.850 --> 01:18:53.460
my question when you were
answering Paul's earlier.

01:18:53.460 --> 01:18:56.090
But just to say,

01:18:56.090 --> 01:18:58.900
I was just thinking of real subsumption

01:18:58.900 --> 01:19:03.000
as the way in which
the reproductive sphere

01:19:03.000 --> 01:19:05.330
the life-making sphere
is already determined

01:19:05.330 --> 01:19:06.163
by what's happening

01:19:06.163 --> 01:19:10.640
and where capital more
directly has an effect.

01:19:10.640 --> 01:19:11.710
And I was thinking about the

01:19:11.710 --> 01:19:14.950
the later chapters in "Capital",

01:19:14.950 --> 01:19:17.390
where you go from formal
subsumption to real

01:19:17.390 --> 01:19:21.040
subsumption is once capital has

01:19:21.040 --> 01:19:23.030
become a totalizing system that

01:19:23.030 --> 01:19:26.240
affects absolutely everything
and everything is read

01:19:26.240 --> 01:19:29.900
through that lens, even
life-making activities.

01:19:29.900 --> 01:19:32.793
So I thought that would perhaps,

01:19:34.985 --> 01:19:37.330
what has been rendered as somewhat

01:19:37.330 --> 01:19:40.940
of a more pessimistic Marx in some regard.

01:19:40.940 --> 01:19:44.250
But I think I was just wondering
if you had any thoughts

01:19:44.250 --> 01:19:47.653
on how that could affect the potential,

01:19:47.653 --> 01:19:50.460
the revolutionary potential

01:19:50.460 --> 01:19:53.970
of these fears that are
less directly controlled

01:19:53.970 --> 01:19:55.033
by wage labor?

01:19:57.610 --> 01:19:58.790
- No, that's a great question.

01:19:58.790 --> 01:20:00.640
Thank you, Vanessa.

01:20:00.640 --> 01:20:04.430
So I'm gonna start, I'm
gonna try to answer that

01:20:04.430 --> 01:20:09.430
with the lyrics of a union
song from the earlier part

01:20:10.367 --> 01:20:15.367
of the 20th century and the
American Union Movement.

01:20:15.864 --> 01:20:19.210
It's a song that I find fascinating

01:20:19.210 --> 01:20:23.080
because there is a recognition
in the early union movement

01:20:23.080 --> 01:20:24.680
of the unity of these spheres

01:20:24.680 --> 01:20:26.020
that you're talking about, right?

01:20:26.020 --> 01:20:30.360
So this song goes, and
I will not subject you

01:20:31.230 --> 01:20:36.230
to my singing, but the song
goes, it's a strike song.

01:20:36.470 --> 01:20:39.640
And it's supposedly from the workers

01:20:39.640 --> 01:20:44.310
and it says something like my children

01:20:44.310 --> 01:20:46.789
are seven and numbers and something

01:20:46.789 --> 01:20:51.163
and they're all starving,
but the refrain is striking,

01:20:52.650 --> 01:20:56.470
striking we're striking
for pairs of shoes.

01:20:56.470 --> 01:20:58.850
And then then there is another description

01:20:58.850 --> 01:21:02.470
of all the things that
the family is deprived of.

01:21:02.470 --> 01:21:04.950
And then it says striking, striking,

01:21:04.950 --> 01:21:06.820
we're striking for bottles of milk.

01:21:06.820 --> 01:21:09.730
So in other words,

01:21:09.730 --> 01:21:14.670
there's a recognition
in the actual struggle

01:21:14.670 --> 01:21:18.200
which is your world of
real subsumption, right?

01:21:18.200 --> 01:21:21.280
Where everything is under
the dominance of capital

01:21:21.280 --> 01:21:25.420
but the reason why you are engaged

01:21:25.420 --> 01:21:28.380
in wage labor is not for money,

01:21:28.380 --> 01:21:30.926
but for what money can buy.

01:21:30.926 --> 01:21:34.810
So that so in other words,

01:21:34.810 --> 01:21:37.510
the relationship between wage labor

01:21:38.560 --> 01:21:43.560
and life is constant and
is actually not ruptured

01:21:45.040 --> 01:21:47.420
in the consciousness of the worker,

01:21:47.420 --> 01:21:51.003
where it gets ruptured is in later,

01:21:52.188 --> 01:21:55.100
both the left movement

01:21:55.100 --> 01:21:56.560
as well as the union movement,

01:21:56.560 --> 01:22:00.570
where we see that in the workplace,

01:22:00.570 --> 01:22:03.060
we are fighting for wages.

01:22:03.060 --> 01:22:05.920
Well, actually let's reverse that idea

01:22:05.920 --> 01:22:08.560
and say, yes, we're fighting for wages

01:22:08.560 --> 01:22:11.950
but we're actually fighting
for an enhancement of life.

01:22:11.950 --> 01:22:14.200
That's why we want higher wages, right?

01:22:14.200 --> 01:22:18.387
Because we want more relaxation time.

01:22:20.110 --> 01:22:21.440
We want better healthcare.

01:22:21.440 --> 01:22:24.100
We want better food for our families.

01:22:24.100 --> 01:22:27.352
So that union song, actually for me

01:22:27.352 --> 01:22:31.970
unites these two spheres
in a very beautiful way

01:22:31.970 --> 01:22:34.380
because there's a
recognition in the early part

01:22:34.380 --> 01:22:36.532
of the union movement in this

01:22:36.532 --> 01:22:40.300
country that the two spheres
are intimately connected.

01:22:40.300 --> 01:22:44.060
And we are actually all fighting
for life-making activities

01:22:44.060 --> 01:22:46.830
and the expansion of
life-making activities.

01:22:46.830 --> 01:22:51.830
And in fact, the wage
is an unnatural shadow

01:22:52.063 --> 01:22:55.238
that falls between ourself

01:22:55.238 --> 01:22:59.203
and our means of sustaining that set.

01:22:59.203 --> 01:23:00.460
There should not be

01:23:00.460 --> 01:23:04.260
that unnatural divide
between us and our means

01:23:04.260 --> 01:23:07.382
of subsistence, but the wage
is an artificial barrier

01:23:07.382 --> 01:23:12.382
between those two, which is why one of,

01:23:15.207 --> 01:23:19.600
the young SRT theorists

01:23:19.600 --> 01:23:21.850
Aaron Jaffe has written recently

01:23:21.850 --> 01:23:25.380
written a short essay where he talks

01:23:25.380 --> 01:23:29.810
about the potential of these life

01:23:29.810 --> 01:23:34.440
making explorations under the pandemic,

01:23:34.440 --> 01:23:37.070
where the pandemic has kind

01:23:37.070 --> 01:23:40.650
of agitated the ubiquity

01:23:40.650 --> 01:23:45.260
and the essential nature
of the wage, right?

01:23:45.260 --> 01:23:46.990
That that we constantly go

01:23:46.990 --> 01:23:50.060
to work and we get the
wage and we come home

01:23:50.060 --> 01:23:54.770
with the disruption of things
under under the pandemic

01:23:54.770 --> 01:23:59.500
we can see almost
transparently the unnaturalness

01:23:59.500 --> 01:24:01.410
of the wage, right?

01:24:01.410 --> 01:24:05.200
Because there's been so much
succession in the workplace

01:24:05.200 --> 01:24:07.930
and in the home, we also see what

01:24:07.930 --> 01:24:11.000
is actually essential work
to keep the world going.

01:24:11.000 --> 01:24:13.980
And it's not stockbrokers and bankers

01:24:13.980 --> 01:24:17.700
it is actually life-making
activities of teachers,

01:24:17.700 --> 01:24:22.280
nurses and people in the
whole care home, et cetera.

01:24:22.280 --> 01:24:25.990
So I don't know if this
answers your question

01:24:25.990 --> 01:24:29.068
but I don't think it, the architecture

01:24:29.068 --> 01:24:33.670
of "Capital Volume I"
actually never distracts

01:24:33.670 --> 01:24:38.270
from the fact that under real subsumption

01:24:38.270 --> 01:24:42.790
that there is this sort
of hegemonic monolith that

01:24:42.790 --> 01:24:45.840
is capital that
extinguishes all possibility

01:24:45.840 --> 01:24:46.980
of workers struggle.

01:24:46.980 --> 01:24:50.472
In fact, what it reveals
is this constant dynamic

01:24:50.472 --> 01:24:55.472
of workers trying to win
back life-making resources

01:24:56.037 --> 01:24:59.290
from capital and capital constantly having

01:24:59.290 --> 01:25:02.810
to negotiate as to how much it can give

01:25:02.810 --> 01:25:05.473
and how much it will give,
or is forced to give.

01:25:06.920 --> 01:25:09.420
- [Vanessa] Thank you so
much, that was wonderful.

01:25:11.485 --> 01:25:14.220
- I want to ask a very
short follow-up to that.

01:25:14.220 --> 01:25:16.512
How can you tell in life-making activities

01:25:16.512 --> 01:25:20.923
which ones are for life and
which ones are for capital?

01:25:24.925 --> 01:25:28.057
- That's a really, really
interesting question.

01:25:28.057 --> 01:25:33.057
So this I think, connected to
your earlier question, Paul,

01:25:33.430 --> 01:25:38.430
that while we think
about in concrete labor

01:25:41.740 --> 01:25:44.430
and concretely laboring bodies,

01:25:44.430 --> 01:25:49.120
we can never forget that
we live under the shadow

01:25:49.120 --> 01:25:53.890
of a systemic reproduction
of abstract labor, right?

01:25:53.890 --> 01:25:54.930
So well,

01:26:00.800 --> 01:26:02.143
kissing your loved one

01:26:03.130 --> 01:26:07.172
is a life-making activity you can often

01:26:07.172 --> 01:26:11.370
finding time to do so is part

01:26:11.370 --> 01:26:16.370
of what makes it difficult sometimes.

01:26:17.047 --> 01:26:22.047
But what is life-making activity?

01:26:22.340 --> 01:26:26.968
I think there the problem,

01:26:26.968 --> 01:26:31.860
the fact that you could
even ask that question

01:26:31.860 --> 01:26:34.452
should alert us to the fact that

01:26:34.452 --> 01:26:38.880
through historically life-making

01:26:38.880 --> 01:26:42.960
activities are continuously
commodified, right?

01:26:42.960 --> 01:26:47.960
So, just like domestic labor
is actually commodified.

01:26:50.430 --> 01:26:54.100
if you have enough money
as a woman, you can get

01:26:54.100 --> 01:26:56.100
away with not doing a jot

01:26:56.100 --> 01:27:00.200
of domestic labor and actually farming it

01:27:00.200 --> 01:27:04.330
out to immigrant women of
color who will come and sweep

01:27:04.330 --> 01:27:07.630
and dust and clean and do
whatever it is in your home.

01:27:07.630 --> 01:27:10.194
And you wouldn't have to
do a bit of work, right?

01:27:10.194 --> 01:27:14.320
So capital will constantly,

01:27:14.320 --> 01:27:18.460
as we know, will try to
commodify every element

01:27:20.200 --> 01:27:24.010
even in the sphere of life-making, right.

01:27:24.010 --> 01:27:29.010
However, this does not finally,
I want to constantly go back

01:27:30.854 --> 01:27:34.910
to the idea that that does not mean

01:27:34.910 --> 01:27:37.220
that it always succeeds,

01:27:37.220 --> 01:27:39.980
that that charge and that dynamic

01:27:39.980 --> 01:27:42.700
of concrete labor continuously remains

01:27:42.700 --> 01:27:44.766
and actually has to remain

01:27:44.766 --> 01:27:49.023
if capital has to has to survive.

01:27:51.540 --> 01:27:56.540
So women or female marked
bodies have to give birth.

01:27:58.001 --> 01:28:00.700
Sometimes they'll give birth

01:28:00.700 --> 01:28:04.330
under certain kinds of
historical conditions

01:28:04.330 --> 01:28:08.320
and other times under
others, but those sorts

01:28:08.320 --> 01:28:11.640
of processes have to
continuously take place.

01:28:11.640 --> 01:28:16.368
And it actually isn't
capitalist's worth to actually

01:28:16.368 --> 01:28:21.160
factory farm an entire
generation of workers, right?

01:28:21.160 --> 01:28:25.030
It just isn't the kind of
investment it has to make

01:28:25.030 --> 01:28:29.410
in order to do that in
changing social mores

01:28:29.410 --> 01:28:32.720
in creating such factory
farms, it just isn't worth it.

01:28:32.720 --> 01:28:36.726
I mean, it gets this
work done very cheaply,

01:28:36.726 --> 01:28:39.890
free of cost in most
working class households.

01:28:39.890 --> 01:28:41.040
Why should it invest

01:28:41.040 --> 01:28:45.860
in that kind of fully auto,

01:28:45.860 --> 01:28:50.860
well, again, you cannot
fully automate care.

01:28:51.059 --> 01:28:53.190
I mean, this is the big thing, right?

01:28:53.190 --> 01:28:58.190
So they try to mechanically
reproduce care,

01:28:58.280 --> 01:29:01.350
especially in the
privatized medical industry.

01:29:01.350 --> 01:29:04.070
And we see the problems with that, right?

01:29:04.070 --> 01:29:06.856
So when you say that

01:29:06.856 --> 01:29:09.170
there are little beepers that the

01:29:09.170 --> 01:29:13.842
nurse will only stay by your
bedside for five minutes

01:29:13.842 --> 01:29:18.700
and has to take the take on on the child.

01:29:18.700 --> 01:29:21.600
And the nurse has to complete

01:29:22.760 --> 01:29:26.470
X number of bed visits per day.

01:29:26.470 --> 01:29:28.165
It just doesn't work.

01:29:28.165 --> 01:29:31.109
In the sense that care cannot be

01:29:31.109 --> 01:29:36.109
mechanized in that extent.

01:29:36.690 --> 01:29:40.710
And that's a problem that
capital has as it were.

01:29:42.870 --> 01:29:44.447
- So I'm gonna tell my partner that

01:29:44.447 --> 01:29:46.741
she's not kissing me just
to make sure I'm home

01:29:46.741 --> 01:29:48.898
at the end of the day to make dinner.

01:29:48.898 --> 01:29:50.981
(laughs)

01:29:52.218 --> 01:29:54.850
Now is a good moment to, I think

01:29:54.850 --> 01:29:57.380
turn to Gabriel Wyman's question.

01:29:57.380 --> 01:30:00.550
I don't know, Gabriel, I
think just maybe left the room

01:30:00.550 --> 01:30:03.130
but if Gabriel is here,
this was a question

01:30:03.130 --> 01:30:06.673
about the commodification
of reproductive labor.

01:30:07.510 --> 01:30:10.793
Can we open up Gabriel's voice?

01:30:15.237 --> 01:30:17.290
Gabriel is here but on the phone,

01:30:17.290 --> 01:30:19.851
but we should still be able to unmute.

01:30:19.851 --> 01:30:22.251
Gabriel, would you like
me to read the question?

01:30:27.830 --> 01:30:28.663
Okay.

01:30:29.700 --> 01:30:32.850
This is the most bizarre way
to communicate with anyone.

01:30:32.850 --> 01:30:34.270
Thanks for a great talk,

01:30:34.270 --> 01:30:35.460
Gabriel says, it strikes me

01:30:35.460 --> 01:30:37.430
you mainly consider SRT in relation

01:30:37.430 --> 01:30:39.636
to its input to the value of labor power,

01:30:39.636 --> 01:30:42.670
as itself an un-commodified form of labor.

01:30:42.670 --> 01:30:45.140
I'm curious what you make of
the growing commodification

01:30:45.140 --> 01:30:48.220
of reproductive labor, which
you just began to talk about,

01:30:48.220 --> 01:30:50.340
which often occurs
through the social state

01:30:50.340 --> 01:30:52.890
in partial or limited commodified forms.

01:30:52.890 --> 01:30:55.080
What is the significance of
the absorption of so much

01:30:55.080 --> 01:30:57.880
of this labor into the labor
market in the last 50 years?

01:30:57.880 --> 01:31:00.020
And what should we make
of the historical limits

01:31:00.020 --> 01:31:03.703
to capital that have
arisen in its employment?

01:31:12.090 --> 01:31:17.090
- So I think by historical limits,

01:31:18.232 --> 01:31:21.770
I think that's what you talked about,

01:31:21.770 --> 01:31:25.080
that you can't mechanize care completely.

01:31:25.080 --> 01:31:26.860
So you can commodify domestic labor

01:31:32.291 --> 01:31:34.800
and the reproduction of
life to a certain extent

01:31:34.800 --> 01:31:38.260
but you cannot mechanize
or commodify it entirely.

01:31:38.260 --> 01:31:41.680
And so that would be my first kind of

01:31:41.680 --> 01:31:44.763
kind of response to you.

01:31:46.130 --> 01:31:50.052
The other side of it is that when you,

01:31:50.052 --> 01:31:55.052
I mean, my friend and
colleague in UMass Amherst

01:31:55.899 --> 01:31:59.700
Nancy Fulvert, who's a feminist economist.

01:31:59.700 --> 01:32:02.043
She talks about, for instance

01:32:02.043 --> 01:32:05.910
one of the problems of workers in

01:32:05.910 --> 01:32:08.500
the care industry striking, right?

01:32:08.500 --> 01:32:11.490
So she calls it the love problem.

01:32:11.490 --> 01:32:13.360
So what's the love problem?

01:32:13.360 --> 01:32:18.360
That often, especially for
home health care workers,

01:32:21.000 --> 01:32:22.490
those who come into the home to care

01:32:22.490 --> 01:32:24.020
for the elderly, et cetera,

01:32:24.020 --> 01:32:26.820
who, by the way, did the majority

01:32:26.820 --> 01:32:31.070
of the caring for our
elders during this pandemic

01:32:31.070 --> 01:32:33.776
and were primarily women of color.

01:32:33.776 --> 01:32:37.000
And so we're absolutely

01:32:37.000 --> 01:32:39.699
at the sort of the full blast

01:32:39.699 --> 01:32:44.000
of the of the disease, of the virus.

01:32:44.000 --> 01:32:47.583
So what happens with
home health care workers?

01:32:48.988 --> 01:32:53.988
Because care is such a sort of human,

01:32:57.250 --> 01:33:02.250
I want to say human and
indeed anticapitalist impulse.

01:33:02.750 --> 01:33:05.160
Care and love are human

01:33:05.160 --> 01:33:09.510
and anticapitalist impulses
that we carry as a species.

01:33:09.510 --> 01:33:12.159
So even when you commodify it,

01:33:12.159 --> 01:33:16.787
it actually creates
certain problems, right?

01:33:17.950 --> 01:33:20.732
So for instance, with a
home health care worker

01:33:20.732 --> 01:33:25.230
she gets so involved in the care

01:33:25.230 --> 01:33:27.240
of her patient, of her client,

01:33:27.240 --> 01:33:31.100
the person she's providing care for,

01:33:31.100 --> 01:33:33.973
that when it comes to striking,

01:33:34.910 --> 01:33:37.620
she feels well I can't strike

01:33:37.620 --> 01:33:39.380
because this will leave

01:33:39.380 --> 01:33:43.200
this really vulnerable
person high and dry,

01:33:43.200 --> 01:33:46.690
and this actually will
affect their life chances.

01:33:46.690 --> 01:33:48.170
So how can I strike?

01:33:48.170 --> 01:33:49.223
Right?

01:33:49.223 --> 01:33:54.223
So this is something that
care workers face all the time

01:33:54.270 --> 01:33:56.690
in terms of striking.

01:33:56.690 --> 01:33:59.570
That whenever as we all know,

01:33:59.570 --> 01:34:02.340
there was a wonderful wave

01:34:02.340 --> 01:34:07.340
of teacher's strike in this
country two years ago in 2017.

01:34:09.160 --> 01:34:10.800
The first thing was

01:34:10.800 --> 01:34:13.397
oh, how could you be
so selfish and strike?

01:34:13.397 --> 01:34:17.287
Because this affects the lives
and futures of our children.

01:34:17.287 --> 01:34:18.193
Okay.

01:34:19.035 --> 01:34:21.730
So when nurses go on strike,
how can you possibly strike?

01:34:21.730 --> 01:34:24.300
Because you are endangering the health,

01:34:24.300 --> 01:34:29.300
the very life, the messy,
fleshy life of human beings.

01:34:29.870 --> 01:34:34.170
So care work at this kind of,

01:34:34.170 --> 01:34:39.080
so in other words, it is not
just care workers commodified,

01:34:39.080 --> 01:34:42.990
but the impulse to care
is often weaponized

01:34:42.990 --> 01:34:45.893
by capital to actually stop care workers

01:34:45.893 --> 01:34:48.090
from striking, right?

01:34:48.090 --> 01:34:50.470
So that very impulse that makes

01:34:50.470 --> 01:34:53.870
us human capital weaponizes against us.

01:34:53.870 --> 01:34:56.373
So you can strike because
you ought to care.

01:34:57.440 --> 01:35:00.290
I think my answer to that,

01:35:00.290 --> 01:35:03.890
I learned actually from
concrete worker struggles.

01:35:03.890 --> 01:35:08.890
So workers then had to devise a

01:35:08.890 --> 01:35:11.977
way to actually frame their struggle,

01:35:11.977 --> 01:35:16.977
which shows that they're
striking because they care.

01:35:17.200 --> 01:35:22.200
So it is because they
care for the future lives

01:35:22.254 --> 01:35:25.300
of their students, of their patients, so

01:35:25.300 --> 01:35:29.210
that they may care
properly and sustainably

01:35:29.210 --> 01:35:30.540
that they are striking.

01:35:30.540 --> 01:35:34.820
So in other words in all
of these care work strikes

01:35:34.820 --> 01:35:39.075
that you, if you follow,
as I do, you will see

01:35:39.075 --> 01:35:42.415
that workers reframe the struggle

01:35:42.415 --> 01:35:45.850
into actually talking about the depths

01:35:45.850 --> 01:35:47.986
of care that they have for their patients

01:35:47.986 --> 01:35:51.121
and for their students,

01:35:51.121 --> 01:35:54.634
which is why they're forced to strike.

01:35:54.634 --> 01:35:59.634
So I remember 2012 the
Chicago Teacher's Union

01:36:00.800 --> 01:36:02.890
when they went for strike that was like,

01:36:02.890 --> 01:36:05.870
you know the first
teacher's strike in so many,

01:36:05.870 --> 01:36:07.630
so many years of recent memory,

01:36:07.630 --> 01:36:09.420
and it was such a great strike.

01:36:09.420 --> 01:36:12.710
And remember that for the Chicago teachers

01:36:12.710 --> 01:36:15.493
80% of their students
are students of color.

01:36:17.054 --> 01:36:22.054
And the Mayor of Chicago
at the time closed schools

01:36:22.160 --> 01:36:24.678
mostly on the South Side of Chicago where

01:36:24.678 --> 01:36:27.660
all the black children

01:36:27.660 --> 01:36:30.719
and Latino children went to school, right?

01:36:30.719 --> 01:36:33.210
But the framing I've thought

01:36:33.210 --> 01:36:36.020
of this Chicago teacher's
strike was so wonderful

01:36:36.020 --> 01:36:39.180
said that our working conditions are our

01:36:39.180 --> 01:36:41.610
students' learning conditions, right?

01:36:41.610 --> 01:36:45.150
So in other words, unless
our working conditions

01:36:45.150 --> 01:36:47.940
improve we are not able to love

01:36:47.940 --> 01:36:51.433
and care for our students in
the way our students deserve.

01:36:52.356 --> 01:36:57.356
So it was a very interesting,
to me, learning process

01:36:57.420 --> 01:37:00.580
for us to see how
workers in struggled just

01:37:00.580 --> 01:37:04.240
as Marx predicted, actually
can reframe the struggle

01:37:04.240 --> 01:37:08.480
for care, into talking
about real substantive care,

01:37:08.480 --> 01:37:11.070
rather than the weaponized care

01:37:11.070 --> 01:37:12.893
that capital forces upon them.

01:37:16.005 --> 01:37:17.600
- It makes me think of a beautiful

01:37:17.600 --> 01:37:19.710
thing that tends to happen in strikes

01:37:19.710 --> 01:37:21.540
and an in group actions that there's

01:37:22.462 --> 01:37:25.090
a kind of care that
arises among the strikers.

01:37:25.090 --> 01:37:26.380
That's really very different

01:37:26.380 --> 01:37:30.110
than anything we're talking
about, kind of the ideal.

01:37:30.110 --> 01:37:32.420
We have a set of questions,

01:37:32.420 --> 01:37:36.435
and I'm mindful of your labor power.

01:37:36.435 --> 01:37:38.113
- That's fine.

01:37:39.020 --> 01:37:41.100
- So we'll go a little longer?

01:37:41.100 --> 01:37:43.493
You okay?
- Sure, absolutely.

01:37:47.732 --> 01:37:49.670
That's a great question.

01:37:49.670 --> 01:37:54.670
Because when we say we want
more public service, right?

01:37:56.350 --> 01:38:00.963
We want more state intervention

01:38:05.474 --> 01:38:07.070
in the market.

01:38:07.070 --> 01:38:10.400
So we want these services
taken off the market,

01:38:10.400 --> 01:38:15.400
taken off private privatized
sphere and into public control.

01:38:16.930 --> 01:38:19.120
So that sounds like a great demand

01:38:19.120 --> 01:38:21.354
that we are saying that it should,

01:38:21.354 --> 01:38:23.150
healthcare should be public,

01:38:23.150 --> 01:38:25.070
or education should be public.

01:38:25.070 --> 01:38:27.660
We should have public laundries and so on.

01:38:27.660 --> 01:38:30.420
But we are also saying at the same time

01:38:30.420 --> 01:38:34.000
if we go via Foucault,
we're also saying that

01:38:34.000 --> 01:38:36.300
then that means that the capitalist state

01:38:36.300 --> 01:38:38.380
is actually in charge of this.

01:38:38.380 --> 01:38:42.836
So, which ought to be a
scary prospect, right?

01:38:42.836 --> 01:38:47.836
So we are saying healthcare,
education should be public,

01:38:49.210 --> 01:38:53.360
but the word public
actually hides in a way,

01:38:53.360 --> 01:38:56.000
the idea that what we mean by public

01:38:56.000 --> 01:38:57.290
is also the capitalist state

01:38:57.290 --> 01:39:00.432
because we were asking for
state control as it were.

01:39:00.432 --> 01:39:04.480
So I think that's a very interesting

01:39:04.480 --> 01:39:08.355
and important issue that
we ought to be discussing.

01:39:08.355 --> 01:39:10.150
What are the limit?

01:39:10.150 --> 01:39:12.580
And this is where I think Marxist

01:39:12.580 --> 01:39:15.332
state theory is something that we have to

01:39:15.332 --> 01:39:18.180
this should be like the sort

01:39:18.180 --> 01:39:21.460
of next chapter of Social
Reproduction Theory.

01:39:21.460 --> 01:39:24.140
Like how does Social
Reproduction Theory engage

01:39:24.140 --> 01:39:26.600
with Marxist state theory?

01:39:26.600 --> 01:39:29.850
Because I think it has to in in a way,

01:39:29.850 --> 01:39:33.050
because the state is both the repository

01:39:33.050 --> 01:39:36.020
of the disciplining functions of capital

01:39:36.020 --> 01:39:37.975
as well as the public functions

01:39:37.975 --> 01:39:41.250
of the reproduction of life.

01:39:41.250 --> 01:39:43.633
So for instance,

01:39:47.255 --> 01:39:48.633
when this early,

01:39:49.840 --> 01:39:53.623
I don't know if you're
in Paul's class, Emerson,

01:39:56.990 --> 01:39:59.963
but in "Capital Volume
I", Marx, as I said,

01:40:01.815 --> 01:40:06.815
is absolutely full of praise

01:40:08.350 --> 01:40:11.500
for the government inspectors who come

01:40:11.500 --> 01:40:15.050
into the factories and
inspect those conditions.

01:40:15.050 --> 01:40:17.040
You know, he goes through
all these blue books

01:40:17.040 --> 01:40:21.080
of the inspectors and
and when he writes his,

01:40:21.080 --> 01:40:23.980
especially his chapter in the working day.

01:40:23.980 --> 01:40:27.150
And that is where we are talking

01:40:27.150 --> 01:40:32.150
about that even in the
sphere of production,

01:40:32.620 --> 01:40:36.170
in the point of production
question that there

01:40:36.170 --> 01:40:41.170
Marx is a welcoming state oversight

01:40:41.390 --> 01:40:45.950
over simple operation of
private capital, right?

01:40:45.950 --> 01:40:48.530
That it's only the capitalist state

01:40:48.530 --> 01:40:51.610
that steps in to actually regulate

01:40:51.610 --> 01:40:56.230
how wages are determined
on the factory floor.

01:40:56.230 --> 01:40:57.890
And the fact that capital

01:40:57.890 --> 01:41:00.870
is actually undermining its
own condition of possibility.

01:41:00.870 --> 01:41:02.970
So it's the capitalist state steps

01:41:02.970 --> 01:41:05.150
in and says, okay, you need to stop.

01:41:05.150 --> 01:41:07.870
And we need to reconfigure
the whole thing here.

01:41:07.870 --> 01:41:09.760
And so labor laws are passed

01:41:09.760 --> 01:41:12.610
and so on in the course of work,

01:41:12.610 --> 01:41:15.880
but it's the same today, right?

01:41:15.880 --> 01:41:18.350
So we are asking for

01:41:20.871 --> 01:41:22.770
the government to step in

01:41:22.770 --> 01:41:26.020
to regulate healthcare or education

01:41:26.020 --> 01:41:28.923
as it does in several Nordic countries.

01:41:29.800 --> 01:41:32.670
But we cannot forget that it is

01:41:32.670 --> 01:41:34.203
the capitalist state, right?

01:41:36.532 --> 01:41:39.130
So we are not talking about

01:41:39.130 --> 01:41:41.780
workers control over the
process of production.

01:41:41.780 --> 01:41:44.830
So the capitalist state
will impose the law of

01:41:48.590 --> 01:41:52.960
capital or will impose the law
of value to a certain extent.

01:41:52.960 --> 01:41:55.850
And we cannot expect otherwise from it

01:41:55.850 --> 01:41:59.380
this is where I think social

01:41:59.380 --> 01:42:02.062
democratic governments will join

01:42:02.062 --> 01:42:06.160
the First World War in 1914, right.

01:42:06.160 --> 01:42:09.870
And shock Lenin and Luxembourg into it.

01:42:09.870 --> 01:42:11.530
This is why social democratic

01:42:11.530 --> 01:42:15.690
governments today are often
virulent practitioners

01:42:15.690 --> 01:42:18.050
of border control,

01:42:18.050 --> 01:42:20.260
that they want strong borders

01:42:20.260 --> 01:42:22.799
against migrants because
they want to control

01:42:22.799 --> 01:42:26.300
the distribution of the wage

01:42:26.300 --> 01:42:28.750
within their own national economies.

01:42:28.750 --> 01:42:33.217
So on the one hand, and this
is why this contradiction

01:42:33.217 --> 01:42:38.217
of the capitalist state
providing life-making activities.

01:42:39.690 --> 01:42:41.390
On the one hand we celebrate

01:42:41.390 --> 01:42:43.817
we absolutely want free healthcare,

01:42:43.817 --> 01:42:48.817
free education from our taxes.

01:42:50.130 --> 01:42:52.960
But we cannot forget that the

01:42:52.960 --> 01:42:57.050
state ultimately will also
impose the law of value.

01:42:57.050 --> 01:42:58.390
Because it is the capitalist state,

01:42:58.390 --> 01:43:00.370
it's not a worker's state.

01:43:00.370 --> 01:43:03.030
So for instance, what we see is

01:43:03.030 --> 01:43:07.320
this dark development in recent times

01:43:07.320 --> 01:43:09.630
that in the Nordic countries

01:43:09.630 --> 01:43:14.500
there is there's this argument that yes,

01:43:14.500 --> 01:43:18.780
we have healthcare but it
is only for Swedish workers.

01:43:18.780 --> 01:43:21.294
So all these immigrants need to be driven.

01:43:21.294 --> 01:43:25.300
Yes, we have good free education

01:43:25.300 --> 01:43:27.930
but it is for our lovely white children.

01:43:27.930 --> 01:43:29.530
And not these Muslims.

01:43:29.530 --> 01:43:34.530
I was shocked when I went
to Copenhagen last year

01:43:35.030 --> 01:43:38.220
it seems like a million years
ago that we used to travel.

01:43:38.220 --> 01:43:41.142
So, and the papers where

01:43:41.142 --> 01:43:43.469
the left-wing papers were filled with

01:43:43.469 --> 01:43:48.469
stories of how Muslims were actually

01:43:48.648 --> 01:43:51.990
Muslim immigrants had to
live in ghettos and had

01:43:51.990 --> 01:43:55.194
to follow an entirely different program

01:43:55.194 --> 01:43:58.650
of sending their children
to school and a daycare

01:43:58.650 --> 01:44:02.070
from the the good white citizens

01:44:02.070 --> 01:44:05.630
who were full citizens in the country.

01:44:05.630 --> 01:44:08.170
So we can see that even

01:44:09.693 --> 01:44:14.693
the generalized reproduction
of life-making activities

01:44:15.550 --> 01:44:18.520
which we support under the aegis

01:44:18.520 --> 01:44:21.580
of the capitalist state
can have its own limits

01:44:21.580 --> 01:44:24.370
because it is the capitalist state and not

01:44:25.610 --> 01:44:28.930
workers controlled
cooperatives or what have you.

01:44:28.930 --> 01:44:29.763
- Got it.

01:44:29.763 --> 01:44:33.407
So basically the principle
differences between good and bad,

01:44:33.407 --> 01:44:35.540
you answer mostly in terms of like,

01:44:35.540 --> 01:44:38.670
the fairness of the state of like racism

01:44:38.670 --> 01:44:42.410
and democratic versus
authoritarian tendencies.

01:44:42.410 --> 01:44:45.580
So like we can see social
reproductive demands

01:44:45.580 --> 01:44:47.571
in the service of like straws, right?

01:44:47.571 --> 01:44:49.600
Nazi-ism or whatever.

01:44:49.600 --> 01:44:51.040
So it's like, those are the kinds

01:44:51.040 --> 01:44:52.943
of places we should distinguish then?

01:44:56.190 --> 01:44:59.603
- I'm not sure I understood that.

01:44:59.603 --> 01:45:00.736
- Just that my original question

01:45:00.736 --> 01:45:02.590
was like the principal demands

01:45:02.590 --> 01:45:05.573
between the social reproduction
demands which are disruptive

01:45:05.573 --> 01:45:07.588
versus non-disruptive have taken

01:45:07.588 --> 01:45:10.450
all those other contexts basically.

01:45:10.450 --> 01:45:12.460
- Of course, yes.

01:45:12.460 --> 01:45:15.103
- [Gabriel] Yeah. Thank you.

01:45:15.996 --> 01:45:18.380
- Elena, if you still have the energy,

01:45:18.380 --> 01:45:20.040
give the floor to Paul Reitter

01:45:20.040 --> 01:45:22.520
to ask a final question.

01:45:22.520 --> 01:45:24.170
- Of course.
- Oh goodness.

01:45:24.170 --> 01:45:26.253
That's a lot of pressure after such an-

01:45:26.253 --> 01:45:28.010
- Or a comment, or a comment.

01:45:28.010 --> 01:45:29.257
- Such an incredible talk

01:45:29.257 --> 01:45:31.767
and a wonderful discussion as well.

01:45:31.767 --> 01:45:33.403
Thanks so much.

01:45:34.310 --> 01:45:39.310
I have a question, a couple of questions,

01:45:39.781 --> 01:45:43.632
but I'll limit myself since
the hour is so advanced.

01:45:43.632 --> 01:45:46.163
and just ask this one,

01:45:47.139 --> 01:45:50.080
you started out talking
about or positioning

01:45:51.615 --> 01:45:55.540
your argument in response
to one that suggests

01:45:56.380 --> 01:46:01.380
that the book is really very
much focused on abstract labor

01:46:01.700 --> 01:46:03.670
and that's ultimately
what's at stake here.

01:46:03.670 --> 01:46:06.080
And I thought you did
a really wonderful job

01:46:06.080 --> 01:46:11.080
of debunking that in a theoretical way.

01:46:11.488 --> 01:46:16.090
And I wonder if there's also a more,

01:46:16.090 --> 01:46:19.030
since so much of it is
about the relation between

01:46:19.030 --> 01:46:20.710
the theoretical and the empirical

01:46:20.710 --> 01:46:22.490
or historical and capital,

01:46:22.490 --> 01:46:24.180
if there's an empirical way

01:46:24.180 --> 01:46:27.520
to push back against that as well,

01:46:27.520 --> 01:46:30.498
because it seems to me that outside

01:46:30.498 --> 01:46:32.050
of the working day chapter

01:46:32.050 --> 01:46:34.097
there are just so many places in capital

01:46:34.097 --> 01:46:39.097
where Marx points out the
tendency of the system

01:46:40.460 --> 01:46:45.022
to exploit a particularly
vulnerable population

01:46:45.022 --> 01:46:50.022
and use that to make
conditions generally worse

01:46:52.730 --> 01:46:55.221
or gain a kind of general advantage.

01:46:55.221 --> 01:46:59.740
And so that even if he
doesn't frame this point

01:46:59.740 --> 01:47:04.210
as a kind of in part of
this theoretical argument

01:47:04.210 --> 01:47:08.312
that it emerges as part

01:47:08.312 --> 01:47:10.940
of the argument that he's making,

01:47:10.940 --> 01:47:14.080
that there's a tendency in
the system systematically

01:47:14.080 --> 01:47:15.760
to go after vulnerable populations.

01:47:15.760 --> 01:47:17.960
So that if there's any inequality at all

01:47:17.960 --> 01:47:22.420
among the workers, that
inequality will be exploited.

01:47:22.420 --> 01:47:23.962
And since it's very hard to imagine

01:47:23.962 --> 01:47:28.618
that there could ever be any total lack

01:47:28.618 --> 01:47:31.921
of inequality among the working population

01:47:31.921 --> 01:47:34.498
under capitalism and its various pressures

01:47:34.498 --> 01:47:39.498
that it's therefore seems very unlikely

01:47:41.110 --> 01:47:42.960
that this book would be suggesting

01:47:42.960 --> 01:47:46.470
that these inequalities
are the inequalities

01:47:46.470 --> 01:47:47.730
other than class inequalities

01:47:47.730 --> 01:47:51.210
or somehow incidental to
capitalist exploitation.

01:47:51.210 --> 01:47:53.330
But there's like there's an accumulation

01:47:53.330 --> 01:47:55.670
of evidence that you could
present, in other words,

01:47:55.670 --> 01:47:56.840
in response to this,

01:47:56.840 --> 01:48:00.226
in addition to the theoretical
argument that you're making.

01:48:00.226 --> 01:48:01.800
- Oh, I completely agree.

01:48:01.800 --> 01:48:04.770
And I think when I say that

01:48:09.130 --> 01:48:14.130
SRT takes issues with certain formulations

01:48:14.740 --> 01:48:19.040
in the way labor power
is presented in capital,

01:48:19.040 --> 01:48:24.040
I mean I always couch that in saying

01:48:24.620 --> 01:48:28.640
in the general framework
that SRT only exists

01:48:28.640 --> 01:48:31.750
as a theory using "Capital Volume I"

01:48:31.750 --> 01:48:33.170
as its architecture, right?

01:48:33.170 --> 01:48:36.880
Because it is from from the understanding

01:48:36.880 --> 01:48:40.610
of how labor power is
crucial to the functioning

01:48:40.610 --> 01:48:43.930
of capital and how that has a,

01:48:45.722 --> 01:48:49.700
and how that creates the conditions

01:48:49.700 --> 01:48:53.090
of possibility of the system as a whole

01:48:53.090 --> 01:48:57.160
that the starting point of
that is "Capital Volume I".

01:48:57.160 --> 01:48:58.805
And there are several, several passages

01:48:58.805 --> 01:49:03.386
in the text itself that
point us back constantly

01:49:03.386 --> 01:49:05.420
to this reproduction

01:49:05.420 --> 01:49:08.680
of labor power and its various modalities

01:49:08.680 --> 01:49:11.080
what "Capital Volume I" doesn't do.

01:49:11.080 --> 01:49:15.581
But what Marx himself does in other texts

01:49:15.581 --> 01:49:17.340
is talk about the gendered

01:49:17.340 --> 01:49:20.030
and racialized nature of the reproduction

01:49:20.030 --> 01:49:22.938
of labor power and the effect it has.

01:49:22.938 --> 01:49:26.030
But I completely agree with you

01:49:26.030 --> 01:49:28.950
that the text is actually peppered,

01:49:28.950 --> 01:49:33.950
it's studded with various
issues about the potential

01:49:36.721 --> 01:49:41.721
of concrete labor, of the
potential of the spaces

01:49:46.450 --> 01:49:49.840
of the reproduction of labor
power becoming the sort

01:49:51.629 --> 01:49:54.170
of anticapitalist
resistance to wage labor.

01:49:54.170 --> 01:49:57.960
So, I mean you never get the impression

01:49:57.960 --> 01:50:01.960
through the reading of
the text that abstract,

01:50:01.960 --> 01:50:04.210
the dominance of abstract labor

01:50:04.210 --> 01:50:08.753
is complete or even absolute in it.

01:50:11.466 --> 01:50:14.920
It is that way in the form

01:50:14.920 --> 01:50:17.180
in which the system operates, but you get

01:50:17.180 --> 01:50:20.260
this constant understanding
of the dynamic,

01:50:20.260 --> 01:50:24.692
the pushbacks to it throughout the text.

01:50:24.692 --> 01:50:26.640
- There's frequently this message

01:50:26.640 --> 01:50:31.640
that in some ways, things
getting worse is progress

01:50:31.700 --> 01:50:35.690
because it galvanizes workers resistance

01:50:35.690 --> 01:50:39.440
and it ripens the conditions

01:50:39.440 --> 01:50:42.550
under which some sort of
meaningful change will occur.

01:50:42.550 --> 01:50:44.625
I mean, in the Working Day chapter

01:50:44.625 --> 01:50:46.970
there's so much back and
forth where he points out

01:50:46.970 --> 01:50:51.950
that the the laws that
regulate factory labor,

01:50:51.950 --> 01:50:55.130
that yes, you know, this
is a positive thing.

01:50:55.130 --> 01:50:56.150
On the other hand,

01:50:56.150 --> 01:50:59.903
they've had these negative
unintended consequences

01:50:59.903 --> 01:51:03.190
of intensifying capitalist
production in some ways

01:51:03.190 --> 01:51:05.630
making it even more ferocious
because it has to operate

01:51:05.630 --> 01:51:07.910
within a more limited time framework.

01:51:07.910 --> 01:51:11.920
But at the same time than that very,

01:51:11.920 --> 01:51:14.137
what you could see as a setback

01:51:14.137 --> 01:51:16.650
has the effect in his estimation

01:51:16.650 --> 01:51:20.955
of bringing about
conditions that will hasten

01:51:20.955 --> 01:51:24.077
the advance of the workers movement.

01:51:24.077 --> 01:51:27.617
And so ultimately that
could be a good thing.

01:51:27.617 --> 01:51:31.860
I mean, and this is obviously a discussion

01:51:31.860 --> 01:51:36.060
that could go on because
you're at the point of,

01:51:36.060 --> 01:51:37.640
and this is one of the of course

01:51:37.640 --> 01:51:39.560
one of the challenges
of the book that it is

01:51:39.560 --> 01:51:44.560
so big and diverse, and
there are moments where

01:51:47.830 --> 01:51:51.900
or there is some amount of variation

01:51:51.900 --> 01:51:54.640
among the different parts
and contradictory statements

01:51:54.640 --> 01:51:57.124
or at least contrasting statements.

01:51:57.124 --> 01:51:59.550
And what reading you'll
come up with can depend

01:51:59.550 --> 01:52:01.420
on which parts you choose to emphasize,

01:52:01.420 --> 01:52:03.730
these parts where Marx
seems more optimistic

01:52:03.730 --> 01:52:07.820
or parts where there's a
certain mood of pessimism

01:52:07.820 --> 01:52:12.820
that sets in and to make
your arguments stick.

01:52:14.130 --> 01:52:15.740
If you're gonna argue in this way,

01:52:15.740 --> 01:52:20.640
then you have to really
catalog exhaustively and show

01:52:20.640 --> 01:52:23.200
at the end of the day that
you have a lot of evidence in

01:52:23.200 --> 01:52:25.860
in support for what you're saying.

01:52:25.860 --> 01:52:28.430
And I do think that there's a lot

01:52:28.430 --> 01:52:32.120
of evidence to support
what you were just saying.

01:52:32.120 --> 01:52:37.120
And in any case, I feel like I'm now going

01:52:38.670 --> 01:52:41.420
from the question mode
to the comment mode.

01:52:41.420 --> 01:52:42.530
And I don't want to do that

01:52:42.530 --> 01:52:44.410
because this your presentation.

01:52:44.410 --> 01:52:47.580
So let me pull back and
give you a last word.

01:52:49.125 --> 01:52:50.020
- Oh, that was excellent.

01:52:50.020 --> 01:52:50.853
Thank you.

01:52:50.853 --> 01:52:51.686
No, no, no.

01:52:52.687 --> 01:52:53.520
I wish we had more time.

01:52:53.520 --> 01:52:54.470
I always think that,

01:52:54.470 --> 01:52:58.800
even after two hours of all this talking.

01:52:58.800 --> 01:53:03.800
So I want, obviously this
this is the starting point

01:53:04.300 --> 01:53:07.170
I hope have more
discussions on this subject.

01:53:07.170 --> 01:53:10.240
I just want to frame sort

01:53:10.240 --> 01:53:14.035
of use what you said
as a point of departure

01:53:14.035 --> 01:53:19.010
about the sort of
intensification of accumulation

01:53:19.010 --> 01:53:24.010
urging workers to higher
levels of struggle.

01:53:24.120 --> 01:53:26.580
I just want to use that
as a point of departure

01:53:26.580 --> 01:53:29.150
to say but in our current times,

01:53:29.150 --> 01:53:30.650
I think we have to frame

01:53:30.650 --> 01:53:32.756
that somewhat in the context

01:53:32.756 --> 01:53:35.500
of the ecological crisis, right?

01:53:35.500 --> 01:53:40.500
So we can think about
expanded reproduction

01:53:43.075 --> 01:53:47.850
and extensive drive for accumulation,

01:53:47.850 --> 01:53:50.870
urging worker struggle,
but it may all be too late,

01:53:50.870 --> 01:53:54.260
in the sense that the
planet may have moved,

01:53:54.260 --> 01:53:59.260
or may have moved beyond
a tipping point where such

01:54:00.080 --> 01:54:04.100
struggles will ultimately
not yield the results

01:54:04.100 --> 01:54:08.130
that we we want, that we
may not be able to move

01:54:08.130 --> 01:54:12.310
to an alternative system of capitalism.

01:54:12.310 --> 01:54:17.310
And I think here, I find
Walter Benjamin quite useful

01:54:18.330 --> 01:54:23.330
in thinking about our
current moment in terms of

01:54:24.505 --> 01:54:29.480
the planetary crisis looming over us

01:54:29.480 --> 01:54:33.090
that we think about workers'
struggles more as a break

01:54:33.090 --> 01:54:38.090
upon this hurtling train of accumulation,

01:54:38.986 --> 01:54:42.680
more as a break than as necessarily

01:54:42.680 --> 01:54:45.310
anything else that we have to first stop

01:54:45.310 --> 01:54:49.810
the current sort of constant drive,

01:54:49.810 --> 01:54:51.967
we have to think about workers' struggles,

01:54:51.967 --> 01:54:54.840
because otherwise the train is gonna,

01:54:54.840 --> 01:54:57.070
and unfortunately we are all on the train,

01:54:57.070 --> 01:54:58.235
not just capitalists,

01:54:58.235 --> 01:55:00.450
and the train is gonna hurtle

01:55:00.450 --> 01:55:05.100
beyond everybody's control
and to the common ruin

01:55:05.100 --> 01:55:09.780
of contending classes as
the famous quote goes.

01:55:09.780 --> 01:55:14.150
But I don't want to end on
that extremely dark note.

01:55:14.150 --> 01:55:17.640
I want to end on the note
that that kind of possibility

01:55:18.596 --> 01:55:20.430
of the common wound of contending

01:55:20.430 --> 01:55:23.210
classes is constantly being challenged

01:55:23.210 --> 01:55:25.560
by a new generation of uprising.

01:55:25.560 --> 01:55:29.915
As we saw in over the
summer, the most fierce

01:55:29.915 --> 01:55:31.960
and the most wonderful multi-racial

01:55:31.960 --> 01:55:35.740
uprising that we saw that
challenged the very processes

01:55:35.740 --> 01:55:40.740
of differential life-making
and the children's strikes

01:55:41.087 --> 01:55:45.240
that we saw last year against
ecological devastation.

01:55:45.240 --> 01:55:48.700
So I want to end on that
note and look towards

01:55:48.700 --> 01:55:53.133
those kinds of horizons as are
possibilities for the future.

01:55:55.620 --> 01:55:56.710
- And we thank you for that.

01:55:56.710 --> 01:55:58.300
And for the talk,

01:55:58.300 --> 01:55:59.848
a great round of applause.

01:55:59.848 --> 01:56:01.060
- Thank you so much.

01:56:01.060 --> 01:56:02.480
- Would be happening.

01:56:02.480 --> 01:56:03.680
Thanks again for coming.

